OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026", "title": "Catalan's constant is irrational", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "005", "family_title": "Irrationality of Catalan’s constant", "abstract_html": "We prove that Catalan's constant \\(G=\\sum_{j\\geq0}(-1)^j/(2j+1)^2\\) is irrational.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Catalan’s constant is\n  \\[\n  G=\\beta(2)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^j}{(2j+1)^2},\n  \\qquad\n  \\beta(s)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^j}{(2j+1)^s}\\quad(s&gt;0).\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Theorem 1.1).\nCatalan’s constant is irrational.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(G\\in\\mathbb Q\\). Proposition 1 makes every \\(\\Delta_N\\) rational. By Proposition 4, \\(\\Delta_p\\ne0\\) for every sufficiently large prime \\(p\\). Apply Proposition 3 along these primes:\n\\[\n\\liminf_{\\substack{p\\to\\infty\\\\p\\text{ prime}}}\n\\left(\n\\frac{\\log|\\Delta_p|}{(48p)^2}-\\frac12\\log2\n\\right)&gt;-2.29084.\n\\]\nProposition 9 gives, along the same sequence, an upper limit at most \\(-2.290939875&lt;-2.2909\\). Since\n\\[\n-2.29084&gt;-2.2909,\n\\]\nthis is a contradiction. Therefore \\(G\\) is irrational.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Hyperbolic sectional curvature is normalized to \\(-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Volumes are those of orientable complete finite-volume hyperbolic three-manifolds with exactly two cusps.</p><p>Statement (paper, unnumbered “Hyperbolic volumes” conclusion, first part).\nThe minimum such volume is irrational. In particular, the volumes of the Whitehead-link complement and the \\((-2,3,8)\\)-pretzel-link complement are irrational.</p>", "proof": "<p>Agol’s theorem identifies \\(4G\\) as the minimum volume of an orientable complete finite-volume hyperbolic three-manifold with exactly two cusps, attained by the Whitehead-link and \\((-2,3,8)\\)-pretzel-link complements (paper reference [1], Introduction and Theorem 3.6). Theorem 1 makes \\(4G\\) irrational. Thus the minimum and both link-complement volumes are irrational.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Hyperbolic sectional curvature is normalized to \\(-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An orientable arithmetic hyperbolic three-orbifold defined over \\(\\mathbb Q(i)\\) has a lattice \\(\\Gamma\\leq\\mathrm{PSL}_2(\\mathbb C)\\) which, up to conjugacy, is commensurable with\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma^1(\\mathcal O)=\\mathcal O^1/\\{\\pm1\\},\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mathcal O\\) is a maximal order in a quaternion algebra \\(B/\\mathbb Q(i)\\).</p><p>Statement (paper, unnumbered “Hyperbolic volumes” conclusion, second part).\nEvery such orbifold has irrational volume. In particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}\\bigl(\\mathrm{PSL}_2(\\mathbb Z[i])\\backslash\\mathbb H^3\\bigr)=G/3\n\\]\nis irrational.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(B/\\mathbb Q(i)\\) have finite reduced discriminant \\(\\mathfrak D\\), and let \\(\\mathcal O\\) be a maximal order. The volume formula gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}\\bigl(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal O)\\backslash\\mathbb H^3\\bigr)\n=\\frac G3\\prod_{\\mathfrak p\\mid\\mathfrak D}(N\\mathfrak p-1),\n\\]\nwhere \\(N\\mathfrak p=|\\mathbb Z[i]/\\mathfrak p|\\). This follows by inserting\n\\[\n\\zeta_{\\mathbb Q(i)}(2)\n=\\zeta(2)L(2,\\chi_{-4})=(\\pi^2/6)G\n\\]\ninto the quaternion-algebra volume formula (paper reference [24], Theorem 39.1.13).</p><p>After conjugating, choose a common finite-index subgroup \\(H\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal O)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}(\\Gamma\\backslash\\mathbb H^3)\n=\n\\frac{[\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal O):H]}{[\\Gamma:H]}\n\\operatorname{vol}\\bigl(\\Gamma^1(\\mathcal O)\\backslash\\mathbb H^3\\bigr).\n\\]\nThis is a positive rational multiple of \\(G\\), and is irrational by Theorem 1.</p><p>For the split algebra \\(B=M_2(\\mathbb Q(i))\\) and \\(\\mathcal O=M_2(\\mathbb Z[i])\\), the product is empty, giving\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}\\bigl(\\mathrm{PSL}_2(\\mathbb Z[i])\\backslash\\mathbb H^3\\bigr)=G/3\n\\]\n(paper reference [24], Example 39.1.16).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For each positive integer \\(N\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\begin{gathered}\n  n=48N,\\quad a=11N,\\quad b=7N,\\quad q=g=4N,\\quad h=2N,\\\\\n  L=n+a=n+b+q=59N,\\quad C=L+g=63N,\\\\\n  H=C+h=65N,\\qquad A=a+2g=19N.\n  \\end{gathered}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(f(t)=\\sqrt{1-t^2}\\), with its positive branch on \\((-1,1)\\), and \\(w=t/(1+f)\\). Thus\n  \\[\n  t=\\frac{2w}{1+w^2},\\qquad f=\\frac{1-w^2}{1+w^2}.\n  \\]\n  The involution \\(*\\) sends \\(w\\) to \\(w^{-1}\\), fixes \\(t\\), and sends \\(f\\) to \\(-f\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\leq r&lt;n\\), define\n  \\[\n  R_r=(1-t)^ht^{C-1}w^{r-g},\\qquad\n  P_r=\\frac{R_r+R_r^*}{2},\\qquad\n  D_r=\\frac{t(R_r^*-R_r)}{2f}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For nonnegative integers \\(i,j\\), define\n  \\[\n  M(i,j)=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_0^1\n  \\frac{|t|}{f(t)}\\frac{t^is^j}{1-ts}\\,ds\\,dt,\n  \\qquad\n  Z(i,j)=\\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\frac{t^is^j}{1-ts}\\,ds\\,dt.\n  \\]\n  Extend these expressions bilinearly to polynomial arguments.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(b\\leq j&lt;L\\), let\n  \\[\n  F_j(r)=M(P_r,j)-\\frac32Z(D_r,j),\\qquad 0\\leq r&lt;n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\Delta_N=\n  \\det_{0\\leq r,k&lt;n}\n  \\left(\n  M\\bigl(P_r,s^{b+k}(1-s)^q\\bigr)\n  -\\frac32Z\\bigl(D_r,s^{b+k}(1-s)^q\\bigr)\n  \\right).\n  \\]\n  If \\(F\\) is the \\(n\\times(n+q)\\) matrix of raw columns \\(F_j\\), the matrix defining \\(\\Delta_N\\) is \\(F\\mathcal T\\), where\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal T_{j,k}=(-1)^{j-b-k}\\binom q{j-b-k},\n  \\qquad b\\leq j&lt;L,\\quad 0\\leq k&lt;n.\n  \\]\n  Out-of-range binomial coefficients are zero.</p><p>Statement (paper, Proposition 2.1).\nEvery raw entry \\(F_j(r)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathbb Q+\\mathbb QG\\). Consequently, if \\(G\\in\\mathbb Q\\), then \\(\\Delta_N\\in\\mathbb Q\\) for every positive integer \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 1.2, the coefficient of \\(\\zeta(2)\\) in a raw entry is\n\\[\n\\frac32\\left(\n\\sum_i[t^i]P_r\\,c_{(j-i-1)/2}-[t^j]D_r\n\\right).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\nf(t)^{-1}=\\sum_{l\\geq0}c_lt^{2l},\n\\]\nthis equals\n\\[\n\\frac32[t^j]\\left(\\frac{tP_r}{f}-D_r\\right)=0\n\\]\nby Lemma 1.1 and \\(j&lt;L\\). More explicitly,\n\\[\nF_j(r)=\n\\sum_i[t^i]P_r\\left(M^0(i,j)+4Gc_{(i-j)/2}\\right)\n-\\frac32\\sum_i[t^i]D_rZ^0(i,j).\n\\]\nThe rational arrays and integer filter prove the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the parameters and row functions of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T_d,U_d\\) be the Chebyshev polynomials, normalized by\n  \\[\n  T_0=1,\\quad T_1=x,\\quad U_{-1}=0,\\quad U_0=1,\n  \\]\n  and the recurrence \\(S_{d+1}=2xS_d-S_{d-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Taylor expansions involving \\(f\\) at zero use \\(f(0)=1\\).</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 2, equations (4)–(5)).\nThe row functions \\(P_r,D_r\\) are integer polynomials. With \\(d=|r-g|\\),\n\\[\nP_r=(1-t)^ht^{C-1}T_d(1/t),\\qquad\nD_r=\\operatorname{sgn}(r-g)(1-t)^ht^{C-1}U_{d-1}(1/t),\n\\]\nwhere \\(D_g=0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{supp}P_r\\subseteq\\{A,\\ldots,H-1\\},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{supp}D_r\\subseteq\\{A+1,\\ldots,H-1\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{tP_r}{f}-D_r=\\frac{tR_r}{f}\n=O(t^{C+r-g})=O(t^L).\n\\]\nThe moments in Proposition 1 converge absolutely, including after multiplication by bounded polynomial factors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since\n\\[\n\\frac{w+w^{-1}}2=\\frac1t,\\qquad\n\\frac{w^{-1}-w}2=\\frac ft,\n\\]\nthe standard Laurent expressions for the Chebyshev polynomials give the stated formulas. In particular these are integer polynomials. The inequality \\(d\\leq n-1-g\\) gives the support bounds.</p><p>Moreover \\(w=t/2+O(t^3)\\), and the row definitions give\n\\[\n\\frac{tP_r}{f}-D_r=\\frac{tR_r}{f}\n=O(t^{C+r-g}).\n\\]\nSince \\(C-g=L\\) and \\(r\\geq0\\), this is \\(O(t^L)\\).</p><p>For \\(0&lt;u&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\int_0^1\\frac{ds}{1-us}=\\frac{-\\log(1-u)}u.\n\\]\nThe function\n\\[\n\\frac{-\\log(1-u)}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\n\\]\nis integrable on \\((0,1)\\). This dominates the absolute \\(M\\)-integrand after integration in \\(s\\); the corresponding assertion for \\(Z\\) is weaker. Bounded polynomial factors preserve this domination. It also justifies termwise integration of the geometric series used below.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the moments \\(M,Z\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  c_l=4^{-l}\\binom{2l}{l}\\quad(l\\geq0),\n  \\]\n  and set \\(c_z=0\\) unless \\(z\\) is a nonnegative integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  m_i=\\int_{-1}^{1}t^i\\frac{|t|}{f(t)}\\,dt\n  =\n  \\begin{cases}\n  \\dfrac{2}{(i+1)c_{i/2}},&amp;i\\geq0\\text{ even},\\\\\n  0,&amp;i\\geq0\\text{ odd},\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\qquad m_{-1}=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define rational boundary arrays \\(k_d^\\pm\\) by\n  \\[\n  k_0^-=k_0^+=k_1^+=0,\\qquad k_1^-=2,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  dk_d^-=(d-1)k_{d-2}^-+m_{d-2}+m_{d-1},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  (d-1)k_d^+=(d-2)k_{d-2}^++\\frac2{d-1}\n  \\qquad(d\\geq2).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  M^0(i,j)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  k_{i-j}^--\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{j}\n  \\dfrac{m_{i-j+k-1}}k,&amp;i\\geq j,\\\\{}[2mm]\n  k_{j-i}^+-\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=j-i+1}^{j}\n  \\dfrac{m_{k-(j-i)-1}}k,&amp;i&lt;j,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  with empty sums zero, and use the algebraic convention\n  \\[\n  M^0(-1,j)=k_{j+1}^+\\qquad(j\\geq0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  B_i^{(d)}=\\sum_{k=1}^{i}k^{-d},\\qquad B_0^{(d)}=0,\n  \\]\n  and define\n  \\[\n  Z^0(i,j)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  -B_i^{(2)},&amp;i=j,\\\\\n  \\dfrac{B_i^{(1)}-B_j^{(1)}}{i-j},&amp;i\\ne j.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  H_z^*=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  c_{z/2},&amp;z\\geq0\\text{ even},\\\\\n  \\dfrac1{zc_{(z-1)/2}},&amp;z\\geq1\\text{ odd}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 2.1, equations (9)–(19)).\nFor \\(i,j\\geq0\\),\n\\[\nM(i,j)=M^0(i,j)+4Gc_{(i-j)/2}\n+\\frac32\\zeta(2)c_{(j-i-1)/2},\n\\]\n\\[\nZ(i,j)=Z^0(i,j)+[i=j]\\zeta(2).\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n\\frac{H_{z+2}^*}{H_z^*}=\\frac{z+1}{z+2},\n\\]\nand the rational boundary arrays have the explicit expressions\n\\[\nk_u^-=\n\\begin{cases}\nH_u^*\\displaystyle\\sum_{\\substack{1\\leq z\\leq u\\\\z\\text{ even}}}\n\\dfrac2{z^2(H_z^*)^2},&amp;u\\text{ even},\\\\{}[2mm]\nH_u^*\\displaystyle\\sum_{\\substack{1\\leq z\\leq u\\\\z\\text{ odd}}}\n\\dfrac2z,&amp;u\\text{ odd},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\\[\nk_{u+1}^+=H_u^*\n\\sum_{\\substack{1\\leq z\\leq u\\\\z\\equiv u\\ (2)}}\n\\frac2{z^2H_z^*}\\qquad(u\\geq0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Symmetry gives the odd moments and \\(m_0=2\\). Integration by parts gives\n\\[\n(i+1)m_i=im_{i-2}\\qquad(i\\geq1),\n\\]\nwhich proves the formula for the even moments. Termwise integration gives\n\\[\nM(i,j)=\\sum_{u\\geq0}\\frac{m_{i+u}}{j+u+1},\n\\qquad\nM(i,j)-M(i+1,j+1)=\\frac{m_i}{j+1}.\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(K_d^-=M(d,0)\\) and \\(K_d^+=M(0,d)\\). Their boundary recurrences are\n\\[\ndK_d^-=(d-1)K_{d-2}^-+m_{d-2}+m_{d-1}\\quad(d\\geq2),\n\\qquad K_1^-=2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(d-1)K_d^+=(d-2)K_{d-2}^++\\frac2{d-1}\\quad(d\\geq2).\n\\]\nFor the first recurrence, the moment recurrence gives the termwise identity\n\\[\n\\frac{dm_{d+u}-(d-1)m_{d+u-2}}{u+1}\n=m_{d+u-2}-m_{d+u}.\n\\]\nThe right side telescopes, since \\(m_v\\to0\\). The same identity for \\(d=1\\), with the term multiplied by \\(d-1\\) omitted, gives \\(K_1^-=m_{-1}+m_0=2\\).</p><p>For the last recurrence, shifting the first series by two leaves, for \\(u\\geq2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{(d-1)m_{u-2}-(d-2)m_u}{d+u-1}=m_{u-2}-m_u.\n\\]\nTheir sum is \\(2\\); the remaining boundary term is \\(-2(d-2)/(d-1)\\), giving the result.</p><p>The independent starting values are\n\\[\nK_0^-=K_0^+=4G,\\qquad K_1^+=\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}=\\frac32\\zeta(2).\n\\]\nFor the first, integrate in \\(s\\) and put \\(t=\\sin\\theta\\) on the positive half-interval:\n\\[\nK_0^-=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\n\\log\\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}\\,d\\theta\n=-4\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\log\\tan v\\,dv=4G.\n\\]\nThe last equality follows on setting \\(x=\\tan v\\) and integrating the geometric series for \\((1+x^2)^{-1}\\) against \\(-\\log x\\).</p><p>For the other start,\n\\[\nK_1^+=\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac1{2k^2c_k}.\n\\]\nThe differential equation\n\\[\n(1-x^2)y''-xy'=2,\\qquad y(0)=y'(0)=0,\n\\]\ndetermines the Taylor series\n\\[\n(\\arcsin x)^2=\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{x^{2k}}{2k^2c_k}.\n\\]\nIts nonnegative coefficients allow passage to \\(x=1\\) by monotone convergence, giving \\(\\pi^2/4\\).</p><p>For completeness, integrate\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{\\rho^k\\sin(kx)}k\n=\\operatorname{Im}\\bigl(-\\log(1-\\rho e^{ix})\\bigr),\n\\qquad0&lt;\\rho&lt;1,\n\\]\nover \\(0&lt;x&lt;\\pi\\), and let \\(\\rho\\uparrow1\\). The imaginary part is uniformly bounded and tends to \\((\\pi-x)/2\\), so dominated convergence gives\n\\[\n2\\sum_{k\\text{ odd}}k^{-2}=\\pi^2/4.\n\\]\nThe odd sum is \\(3\\zeta(2)/4\\), establishing the last starting identity.</p><p>The homogeneous parts of the minus and plus boundary recurrences propagate the starts by precisely the two \\(c\\)-kernels in the statement. Diagonal reduction preserves both index differences. Replacing the starts \\(K_0^-=K_0^+\\) and \\(K_1^+\\) by zero therefore gives the stated formula for \\(M^0\\) and the evaluation of \\(M\\).</p><p>For \\(Z\\),\n\\[\nZ(i,j)=\\sum_{u\\geq0}\\frac1{(i+u+1)(j+u+1)}.\n\\]\nPartial fractions when \\(i\\ne j\\), and direct summation when \\(i=j\\), give its evaluation.</p><p>Finally, divide each boundary recurrence by the appropriate \\(H^*\\), using\n\\[\nH_{z+2}^*/H_z^*=(z+1)/(z+2).\n\\]\nFor the minus recurrence the increment is \\(2/(z^2(H_z^*)^2)\\) for even \\(z\\), and \\(2/z\\) for odd \\(z\\); the odd case starts with \\(k_1^-/H_1^*=2\\). For the plus recurrence the increment is \\(2/(z^2H_z^*)\\), with \\(k_1^+=0\\) and \\(k_2^+=2\\). This proves the explicit formulas, including their empty-sum cases.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(G\\in\\mathbb Q\\), also viewed in \\(\\mathbb Q_2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the determinant and parameters of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize \\(v_2(2)=1\\), and put \\(v_2(0)=+\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Constants with subscript \\(G\\) may depend on the fixed hypothesized rational value of \\(G\\), but not on \\(N\\).</p><p>Statement (paper, Proposition 2.2).\nWith\n\\[\n\\delta=\\frac{q+g+h}{n}=\\frac5{24},\n\\]\nfor every positive integer \\(N\\),\n\\[\nv_2(\\Delta_N)\\geq\n-\\left(\\frac\\delta2+\\frac{\\delta^2}{8}\\right)n^2\n-O_G(n\\log(n+2))\n=\n-\\frac{505}{4608}n^2-O_G(n\\log(n+2)).\n\\]\nThe same bound holds for every full minor of the raw column matrix.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand \\(\\det(F\\mathcal T)\\) by Cauchy–Binet. Each term is an integer times a full raw minor with distinct column indices \\(j_1,\\ldots,j_n\\). Fix such a minor and expand its columns by Lemma 2.1.</p><p>Consider a term with \\(m\\) columns of type \\(E\\), \\(l\\) of type \\(B\\), and \\(n-m-l\\) of type \\(S\\). Expand the exceptional columns through the vectors \\(\\mathbf p_u,\\mathbf d_u\\) of Lemma 2.2. Repeated indices within either group give equal vectors and zero determinants. Thus, for nonzero terms,\n\\[\n\\sum_{E\\text{ columns}}u\\geq\\frac{m(m-1)}2,\\qquad\n\\sum_{B\\text{ columns}}u\\geq\\frac{l(l-1)}2.\n\\]\nNo distinctness between these two groups is needed. The fixed distinct raw indices also give\n\\[\n\\sum_{E\\text{ columns}}j\\geq mb+\\frac{m(m-1)}2.\n\\]</p><p>The \\(E\\)-group contributes at least\n\\[\n2\\sum u+\\sum j-Hm-K_Gm.\n\\]\nThe \\(B\\)-group contributes at least\n\\[\n\\sum u-O_G(l\\log(H+2)),\n\\]\nand the smoothed columns contribute at least\n\\[\nC(n-m-l)-O((n-m-l)\\log(H+2)).\n\\]\nHence every term, every raw minor, and the filtered determinant satisfy\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_2(\\Delta_N)\\geq&amp;-O_G(n\\log(n+2))\\\\\n&amp;+\\min_{\\substack{m,l\\geq0\\\\m+l\\leq n}}\n\\left\\{\n-(H-b)m+\\frac32m^2+\\frac12l^2+C(n-m-l)\n\\right\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFinite summation causes no further valuation loss.</p><p>Minimize over real \\(m,l\\), which can only lower the integer minimum. Since \\(C\\geq n\\), the expression decreases as \\(l\\) increases to \\(n-m\\), so take \\(l=n-m\\). Using \\(H-b=n(1+\\delta)\\), the remaining expression is\n\\[\n\\frac{n^2}{2}-(2+\\delta)nm+2m^2\n=\n2\\left(m-\\frac{(2+\\delta)n}{4}\\right)^2\n-\\left(\\frac\\delta2+\\frac{\\delta^2}{8}\\right)n^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\delta=5/24\\), this proves the proposition, including its assertion for every full raw minor.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the rationality assumption and parameters of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(m_i,c_l,M^0,Z^0\\) from Lemma 1.2.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(i,j\\geq0\\), define the \\(2\\)-adic series\n  \\[\n  M_s(i,j)=\\sum_{k\\geq0}\\frac{m_{i+k}}{j+k+1}.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 2.2, equations (22)–(25)).\nThe series converges in \\(\\mathbb Q_2\\), and, for \\(0\\leq i,j&lt;H\\),\n\\[\nv_2(M_s(i,j))\\geq i-2\\log_2H-1.\n\\]\nThere are fixed \\(e_1,e_2\\in\\mathbb Q_2\\), independent of \\(N,i,j\\), given by\n\\[\ne_1=4G-M_s(0,0),\\qquad e_2=-M_s(0,1),\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nM^0(i,j)+4Gc_{(i-j)/2}\n=M_s(i,j)+e_1c_{(i-j)/2}+e_2c_{(j-i-1)/2}.\n\\]\nConsequently, every raw column decomposes as\n\\[\nF_j=S_j+E_j+B_j,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nS_j(r)=M_s(P_r,j),\n\\]\n\\[\nE_j(r)=e_1\\sum_i[t^i]P_rc_{(i-j)/2},\n\\]\n\\[\nB_j(r)=\\sum_i[t^i]D_r\n\\left(e_2[i=j]-\\frac32Z^0(i,j)\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For even \\(u\\), the factorial formula implies\n\\[\nv_2(m_u)=1+u-v_2\\binom u{u/2}\n\\geq u+1-\\log_2(u+1).\n\\]\nEach binary place contributes at most one to the binomial valuation. Odd moments are zero. For \\(0\\leq i,j&lt;H\\), every nonzero summand in \\(M_s(i,j)\\) has valuation at least\n\\[\ni+k+1-2\\log_2(H+k)\n\\geq i+1-2\\log_2H+k-2\\log_2(k+1)\n\\geq i-2\\log_2H-1.\n\\]\nThe middle expression tends to infinity with \\(k\\). Hence the series converges, with the stated uniform bound.</p><p>The diagonal recurrence and both boundary recurrences from Lemma 1.2 hold for \\(M_s\\): the same finite telescoping calculations apply because their tails tend to zero \\(2\\)-adically. Its minus start at one is forced to be \\(2\\). There are therefore exactly two homogeneous discrepancies between \\(M_s\\) and \\(M^0(i,j)+4Gc_{(i-j)/2}\\), giving the displayed formula.</p><p>Contract the last kernel with \\(P_r\\) and use\n\\[\n[t^j](tP_r/f)=[t^j]D_r\\qquad(j&lt;L).\n\\]\nThis gives the raw-column decomposition. If a discrepancy is zero, its summands vanish; no finite valuation need be assigned to a zero constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 2 and Lemma 2.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Remove the common factor \\((1-t)^h\\) and write\n  \\[\n  \\frac{P_r(t)}{(1-t)^h}\n  =\\sum_{u\\geq0}p_{r,u}t^{C-1-u},\n  \\qquad\n  \\frac{D_r(t)}{(1-t)^h}\n  =\\sum_{u\\geq0}d_{r,u}t^{C-1-u}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\mathbf p_u=(p_{r,u})_r\\), \\(\\mathbf d_u=(d_{r,u})_r\\), and \\(L_2=\\lfloor\\log_2H\\rfloor\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a fixed nonnegative integer \\(K_G\\) such that \\(v_2(e_1),v_2(e_2)\\geq-K_G\\).</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 2.2, equations (27)–(29)).\nThe coefficient bounds are\n\\[\nv_2(p_{r,u}),v_2(d_{r,u})\\geq u-1.\n\\]\nEvery entry of \\(S_j\\) has valuation at least \\(C-2\\log_2H-3\\), and\n\\[\nv_2(Z^0(i,j))\\geq-2L_2\\qquad(0\\leq i,j&lt;H).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nE_j=\\sum_u\\mathbf p_u a_{u,j},\\qquad\nB_j=\\sum_u\\mathbf d_u b_{u,j},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\na_{u,j}=e_1\\sum_{v=0}^h(-1)^v\\binom hv\nc_{(C-1-u+v-j)/2},\n\\]\n\\[\nb_{u,j}=\\sum_{v=0}^h(-1)^v\\binom hv\n\\left(\ne_2[C-1-u+v=j]-\\frac32Z^0(C-1-u+v,j)\n\\right),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nv_2(a_{u,j})\\geq u+j-H+1-K_G,\n\\qquad\nv_2(b_{u,j})\\geq-2L_2-1-K_G.\n\\]\nOnly indices with nonzero coefficient vectors are included.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induction in the Chebyshev recurrence shows that the coefficient of \\(x^u\\) in \\(T_d\\) has valuation at least \\(u-1\\), and that in \\(U_d\\) has valuation at least \\(u\\); at \\(u=0\\), integrality suffices. This proves the coefficient bounds. Convolution with \\((1-t)^h\\) uses integer coefficients. Combining these bounds with Lemma 2.1 gives the bound for \\(S_j\\).</p><p>A harmonic sum of order \\(d\\) has valuation at least \\(-dL_2\\). Off the diagonal, division by \\(i-j\\) loses at most another \\(L_2\\). The formula for \\(Z^0\\) therefore gives \\(v_2(Z^0(i,j))\\geq-2L_2\\).</p><p>The expansions follow by substituting the polynomial coefficients into Lemma 2.1. Every moment index occurring is between \\(0\\) and \\(H-1\\). Since \\(v_2(c_l)\\geq-2l\\), each contributing summand gives the stated bounds. The ultrametric inequality introduces no loss for the number of summands or polynomial convolutions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(G\\in\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the determinant \\(\\Delta_N\\), raw columns, and parameters of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For every prime \\(p\\), normalize \\(v_p(p)=1\\), and put \\(v_p(0)=+\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Reduction modulo an odd prime always includes local integrality of both sides.</p><p>Statement (paper, Proposition 3.4).\nAlong any sequence of positive integers \\(N\\to\\infty\\) for which \\(\\Delta_N\\ne0\\),\n\\[\n\\liminf_{N\\to\\infty}\n\\left(\\frac{\\log|\\Delta_N|}{n^2}-\\frac12\\log2\\right)\n\\geq\n-\\frac{8609}{4608}\n-\\left(\\frac12+\\frac{505}{4608}\\right)\\log2\n&gt;-2.29084.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every fixed denominator prime of \\(G\\) eventually lies below \\(2\\sqrt H\\). Thus the preceding large-prime lemmas apply for all sufficiently large \\(N\\).</p><p>Put \\(x=p/N\\). For \\(x\\leq65/2\\), Lemma 3.2 gives\n\\[\n\\frac{d_0}{N}\n=\\bigl(\\min(x,59-x,19)-\\max(7,65-2x)\\bigr)_+.\n\\]\nThis is zero on \\([0,23]\\), \\(2x-46\\) on \\([23,29]\\), and \\(12\\) on \\([29,65/2]\\). Only the part exceeding \\(q/N=4\\) improves a full minor.</p><p>For \\(x&gt;65/2\\), Lemma 3.4 gives\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\nx\\text{ range}&amp;R/N&amp;S/N\\\\ \\hline\n[65/2,40]&amp;105-2x&amp;12\\\\{}\n[40,52]&amp;65-x&amp;52-x\\\\{}\n[52,58]&amp;117-2x&amp;x-52\\\\{}\n[58,59]&amp;59-x&amp;6\\\\{}\n[59,65]&amp;0&amp;65-x\n\\end{array}.\n\\]\nTaking \\(\\min(96,48+R/N,2R/N+S/N)\\) introduces the additional breakpoint \\(69/2\\). Thus\n\\[\nv_p(\\Delta_N)\\geq-Nd(p/N)\\qquad(p&gt;2\\sqrt H),\n\\]\nwhere \\(d(x)=0\\) for \\(x\\geq65\\), and\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\text{range}&amp;d(x)&amp;\\text{endpoint values}&amp;\\int_{\\text{range}}d(x)\\,dx\\\\ \\hline\n[0,25]&amp;96&amp;96,96&amp;2400\\\\{}\n[25,29]&amp;146-2x&amp;96,88&amp;368\\\\{}\n[29,65/2]&amp;88&amp;88,88&amp;308\\\\{}\n[65/2,69/2]&amp;153-2x&amp;88,84&amp;172\\\\{}\n[69/2,40]&amp;222-4x&amp;84,62&amp;803/2\\\\{}\n[40,58]&amp;182-3x&amp;62,8&amp;630\\\\{}\n[58,59]&amp;124-2x&amp;8,6&amp;7\\\\{}\n[59,65]&amp;65-x&amp;6,0&amp;18\n\\end{array}.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\int_0^{65}d(x)\\,dx=\\frac{8609}{2}.\n\\]</p><p>The omitted small odd primes cost \\(o(n^2)\\). At any odd prime,\n\\[\n0\\leq v_p\\binom{2l}{l}\\leq1+\\frac{\\log H}{\\log p}\n\\qquad(2l\\leq H),\n\\]\nsince each factorial-floor difference is zero or one. The explicit formulas of Lemma 1.2 and diagonal reduction give an absolute \\(C_0\\) such that\n\\[\nv_p(F_j(r))\\geq\n-C_0\\left(1+\\frac{\\log H}{\\log p}\\right)\n-v_p(\\operatorname{den}G).\n\\]\nFor instance, each summand of \\(k_u^-\\) uses at most two powers of \\(z\\) and two powers of \\(H_z^*\\) in its denominator. Summation introduces no further loss. Integer row and filter coefficients preserve the bound. Determinant expansion multiplies it by at most \\(n\\), and summing over \\(p\\leq2\\sqrt H\\), with weights \\(\\log p\\), costs\n\\[\nO\\bigl(n\\sqrt H\\log H+n\\log(\\operatorname{den}G)\\bigr)=o(n^2).\n\\]\nOnly the elementary bound on the number of such primes by \\(2\\sqrt H\\) is used. For large \\(N\\), primes \\(p&gt;H\\) are integral throughout, since all factorial and degree-denominator prime factors are at most \\(H\\), and the fixed denominator of \\(G\\) has no prime above \\(H\\).</p><p>Use the prime number theorem\n\\[\n\\vartheta(y):=\\sum_{p\\leq y}\\log p\\sim y.\n\\]\nIts needed weighted consequence is\n\\[\n\\frac1N\\sum_{p\\leq65N}d(p/N)\\log p\n\\longrightarrow\\int_0^{65}d(x)\\,dx.\n\\]\nIndeed, for a fixed partition \\(0=x_0&lt;\\cdots&lt;x_m=65\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\vartheta(Nx_j)-\\vartheta(Nx_{j-1})}{N}\n\\longrightarrow x_j-x_{j-1}.\n\\]\nUpper and lower step functions from the supremum and infimum of \\(d\\) bound the weighted sum. Their limiting difference tends to zero as the mesh decreases, since \\(d\\) is uniformly continuous. Partition endpoints cost \\(O(\\log N/N)\\). Removing \\(2\\) and primes below \\(2\\sqrt H\\) costs \\(o(1)\\), using \\(\\|d\\|_\\infty=96\\) and\n\\[\n\\vartheta(2\\sqrt H)\\leq2\\sqrt H\\log(2\\sqrt H).\n\\]\nSince \\(n^2=2304N^2\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{p\\text{ odd}}v_p(\\Delta_N)\\log p\n\\geq-\\frac{8609}{4608}n^2-o(n^2).\n\\]\nProposition 2 supplies\n\\[\nv_2(\\Delta_N)\\geq-\\frac{505}{4608}n^2-o(n^2).\n\\]</p><p>For a nonzero rational determinant, prime factorization gives the exact identity\n\\[\n\\log|\\Delta_N|=\\sum_pv_p(\\Delta_N)\\log p.\n\\]\nDivide by \\(n^2\\) and subtract \\(\\frac12\\log2\\).</p><p>For the final strict numerical comparison,\n\\[\n\\log2=2\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty\\frac{3^{-(2j+1)}}{2j+1},\n\\]\nand the positive geometric tail gives\n\\[\n\\log2\\leq\n2\\sum_{j=0}^{5}\\frac{3^{-(2j+1)}}{2j+1}\n+\\frac{2\\,3^{-13}}{13(1-3^{-2})}\n&lt;\\frac{693149}{10^6}.\n\\]\nThus the lower constant is strictly larger than\n\\[\n-\\frac{8609}{4608}\n-\\frac{2809}{4608}\\frac{693149}{10^6}\n=-\\frac{10556055541}{4608000000}&gt;-2.29084.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the rationality assumption and parameters of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be an odd prime satisfying \\(2\\sqrt H&lt;p\\leq H\\), and suppose \\(p\\) does not divide the denominator of \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In \\(\\mathbb F_p[t]\\), put\n  \\[\n  E(t)=(1-t^2)^{(p-1)/2}=\\sum_{d=0}^{p-1}E_dt^d,\n  \\qquad \\epsilon=(-1)^{(p-1)/2},\n  \\]\n  and set \\(E_d=0\\) outside \\(0\\leq d&lt;p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the rational arrays of Lemma 1.2. For \\(0\\leq i,j&lt;H\\), set\n  \\[\n  \\ell=j\\bmod p,\\quad d=(j-i-1)\\bmod p,\\quad\n  i'=\\frac{i+1+d-\\ell}{p}-1,\\quad\n  j'=\\left\\lfloor\\frac jp\\right\\rfloor,\n  \\]\n  with \\(0\\leq\\ell,d&lt;p\\).</p><p>Statement (paper, Lemma 3.1, “Digit reductions”).\nOne has \\(i'\\geq-1\\), and, with \\(M^0(-1,j')=k_{j'+1}^+\\),\n\\[\np^2M^0(i,j)\\equiv E_dM^0(i',j')\\pmod p,\n\\]\n\\[\np^2Z^0(i,j)\\equiv\n\\begin{cases}\nZ^0(\\lfloor i/p\\rfloor,j'),&amp;i\\equiv j\\pmod p,\\\\\n0,&amp;i\\not\\equiv j\\pmod p.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(p^2M^0(i,j)\\) and \\(p^2Z^0(i,j)\\) are locally integral.</p>", "proof": "<p>Within \\(0\\leq d&lt;p\\),\n\\[\nE_d=\n\\begin{cases}\nc_{d/2}\\pmod p,&amp;d\\text{ even},\\\\\n0,&amp;d\\text{ odd},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad E_{p-1-d}=\\epsilon E_d.\n\\]\nThe first identity follows from\n\\[\n(-1)^k\\binom{(p-1)/2}{k}\\equiv4^{-k}\\binom{2k}{k}\\pmod p;\n\\]\nthe second follows by reversing coefficients.</p><p>Write \\(z=Pp+r\\), \\(0\\leq r&lt;p\\), \\(0\\leq z&lt;H\\). Since \\(H&lt;p^2/4\\), the factors at reduced indices \\(P\\) are \\(p\\)-adic units. If \\(z\\) is even,\n\\[\nH_z^*\\equiv H_P^*c_{r/2}\\pmod p\\quad(r\\text{ even}),\n\\qquad\nv_p(H_z^*)=1\\quad(r\\text{ odd}).\n\\]\nWhen \\(r\\) is even, \\(P\\) is even, and the base-\\(p\\) digits of \\(z/2\\) are \\(P/2,r/2\\). Expanding \\((1+x)^z\\) modulo \\(p\\) using \\((1+x)^p=1+x^p\\) proves the first assertion, including \\(4^{-z/2}\\). When \\(r\\) is odd, \\(P\\) is odd and the low digit of \\(z/2\\) is \\((p+r)/2\\). The factorial formula has exactly one carry:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac zp\\right\\rfloor\n-2\\left\\lfloor\\frac{z/2}{p}\\right\\rfloor=1,\n\\]\nwith no \\(p^2\\)-contribution. Thus, for positive even \\(z\\), \\(v_p(zH_z^*)\\) is zero or one, and is one exactly when \\(r=0\\) or \\(r\\) is odd.</p><p>For odd \\(z\\),\n\\[\n1/H_z^*\\in\\mathbb Z_p,\\qquad\npH_z^*\\equiv\n\\begin{cases}\n\\epsilon c_{r/2}H_P^*,&amp;r\\text{ even},\\\\\n0,&amp;r\\text{ odd}\n\\end{cases}\n\\pmod p.\n\\]\nIf \\(r\\) is odd, \\(z\\) and \\(c_{(z-1)/2}\\) are units. If \\(r\\) is even, \\(P\\) is odd, and at \\(z=Pp\\),\n\\[\nc_{(Pp-1)/2}\\equiv\nc_{(P-1)/2}c_{(p-1)/2}\n=\\epsilon c_{(P-1)/2},\n\\qquad\npH_{Pp}^*\\equiv\\epsilon H_P^*.\n\\]\nThe recurrence \\(H_{z+2}^*/H_z^*=(z+1)/(z+2)\\), through \\(r=0,2,\\ldots,p-1\\), gives the remaining nonzero cases; its denominators there are units.</p><p>Since \\(m_{z-1}=2H_z^*\\) for odd \\(z\\), and is zero for even \\(z\\),\n\\[\npm_{z-1}\\equiv E_{p-1-r}m_{P-1}\\pmod p.\n\\]\nThis includes \\(z=0\\), with \\(m_{-1}=0\\).</p><p>Next, for \\(u=Pp+r&lt;H\\),\n\\[\np^2k_u^-\\equiv E_{p-1-r}k_P^-,\n\\qquad\np^2k_{u+1}^+\\equiv E_rk_{P+1}^+\n\\pmod p.\n\\]\nFor odd \\(u\\),\n\\[\np^2k_u^-=(pH_u^*)\n\\sum_{\\substack{1\\leq z\\leq u\\\\z\\text{ odd}}}\\frac{2p}{z}.\n\\]\nOnly \\(z=mp\\), with \\(m\\) odd, survives. If \\(r\\) is odd, the first factor vanishes modulo \\(p\\); if \\(r\\) is even, the preceding reduction gives\n\\[\n\\epsilon E_rH_P^*\n\\sum_{\\substack{m\\leq P\\\\m\\text{ odd}}}\\frac2m\n=E_{p-1-r}k_P^-.\n\\]</p><p>For even \\(u\\),\n\\[\np^2k_u^-=\nH_u^*\\sum_{\\substack{1\\leq z\\leq u\\\\z\\text{ even}}}\n2\\left(\\frac p{zH_z^*}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nThe summands are integral, and the expression vanishes modulo \\(p\\) when \\(r\\) is odd. When \\(r\\) is even, \\(P\\) is even. The nonzero summands form complete blocks\n\\[\nz=mp-\\lambda,\\qquad\nm=2,4,\\ldots,P,\\quad\n\\lambda=0,2,\\ldots,p-1.\n\\]\nIndices with odd residue lie immediately below an even multiple \\(mp\\); that multiple supplies \\(\\lambda=0\\). The upper limit includes the whole block ending at \\(Pp\\), and the next block begins at \\((P+1)p+1&gt;u\\).</p><p>Following the recurrence downwards from \\(mp\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac p{(mp-\\lambda)H_{mp-\\lambda}^*}\n\\equiv\\frac{c_{\\lambda/2}}{mH_m^*}\\pmod p.\n\\]\nEach downward step multiplies by \\((z-1)/(z-2)\\), giving the successive factors \\((2j-1)/(2j)\\) modulo \\(p\\). With \\(s=(p-1)/2\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^sc_j^2\n\\equiv\\sum_{j=0}^s\\binom sj^2\n=\\binom{2s}s\\equiv(-1)^s=\\epsilon\\pmod p.\n\\]\nThe equality is the coefficient identity from \\((1+x)^s(1+x)^s\\). Multiplication by \\(H_u^*\\equiv E_rH_P^*\\) proves the minus reduction.</p><p>For the plus reduction and even \\(u\\), use\n\\[\np^2k_{u+1}^+\n=H_u^*\\sum_{\\substack{1\\leq z\\leq u\\\\z\\equiv u\\ (2)}}\n\\frac{2p^2}{z^2H_z^*}.\n\\]\nTerms with \\(p\\nmid z\\) vanish, because \\(v_p(H_z^*)\\leq1\\). At \\(z=mp\\), \\(m\\) is even and \\(H_{mp}^*\\equiv H_m^*\\). This gives \\(E_rk_{P+1}^+\\) for even \\(r\\), and zero for odd \\(r\\). For odd \\(u\\), write the expression as\n\\[\n(pH_u^*)\\sum_{\\substack{1\\leq z\\leq u\\\\z\\text{ odd}}}\n\\frac{2p}{z^2H_z^*}.\n\\]\nThe reciprocal \\(1/H_z^*\\) is integral. Only \\(z=mp\\) survives, where \\(pH_{mp}^*\\equiv\\epsilon H_m^*\\); the two factors \\(\\epsilon\\) cancel. If \\(r\\) is odd, the prefactor vanishes. This proves the starting reductions and their integrality.</p><p>If \\(i\\geq j\\), put \\(u=i-j=Pp+r\\). Then \\(d=p-1-r\\), \\(i'=j'+P\\), and\n\\[\nM^0(i,j)=k_u^--\\sum_{k=1}^j\\frac{m_{u+k-1}}k.\n\\]\nAfter multiplication by \\(p^2\\), terms with \\(p\\nmid k\\) vanish. At \\(k=mp\\), the reduced summand is\n\\[\nE_{p-1-r}\\frac{m_{P+m-1}}m.\n\\]\nThe starting reduction therefore gives \\(E_dM^0(i',j')\\).</p><p>If \\(i&lt;j\\), put \\(u=j-i-1=Pp+r\\). Then \\(d=r\\), \\(i'=j'-P-1\\), and\n\\[\nM^0(i,j)=k_{u+1}^+\n-\\sum_{k=u+2}^j\\frac{m_{k-u-2}}k.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=mp\\),\n\\[\nk-u-1=(m-P-1)p+(p-1-r),\n\\]\nso the reduced summand is \\(E_rm_{m-P-2}/m\\). A possible first multiple with \\(m=P+1\\) contributes \\(m_{-1}=0\\). Thus the sum runs over \\(m=P+2,\\ldots,j'\\), as required. Also\n\\[\ni'=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{i-\\ell}{p}\\right\\rfloor\\geq-1.\n\\]\nWhen \\(i'=-1\\), one has \\(j'=P\\); the sum is empty and the start is \\(k_{j'+1}^+=M^0(-1,j')\\). The reduced indices are less than \\(H/p&lt;p/4\\), and even \\(j'+1&lt;p\\), so all right-hand quantities are integral.</p><p>For \\(Z^0\\), put \\(I=\\lfloor i/p\\rfloor\\), \\(J=\\lfloor j/p\\rfloor\\). Since \\(i,j&lt;p^2\\),\n\\[\npB_i^{(1)}\\equiv B_I^{(1)},\\qquad\np^2B_i^{(2)}\\equiv B_I^{(2)}\\pmod p.\n\\]\nThe diagonal follows immediately. Off the diagonal, if \\(i\\not\\equiv j\\pmod p\\), the denominator \\(i-j\\) is a unit and the reduction is zero. If the residues agree,\n\\[\ni-j=p(I-J),\n\\]\nwith \\(I-J\\) a unit, and substitution gives \\(Z^0(I,J)\\). This also proves integrality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the assumptions on \\(p,N,G\\) and the polynomial \\(E\\) from Lemma 3.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P,D\\) denote the vectors of all row polynomials.</p><p class=\"li\">For a fixed residue \\(0\\leq\\ell&lt;p\\), define vectors over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\) by\n  \\[\n  P_u'=[t^{(u+1)p+\\ell}]\\,tP(t)E(t)\\quad(u\\geq-1),\n  \\qquad\n  D_u'=[t^{up+\\ell}]D(t)\\quad(u\\geq0).\n  \\]\n  Their dependence on \\(\\ell\\) is understood.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(\\ell\\geq H-2p\\), put\n  \\[\n  V_\\ell=2P_1'-\\frac32D_1',\n  \\qquad\n  U_\\ell=2P_{-1}'-\\frac32D_0'.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 3.2, equations (36)–(38)).\nEvery raw column lies in \\(p^{-2}\\mathbb Z_p^n\\), with\n\\[\nX_{kp+\\ell}:=p^2F_{kp+\\ell}\\bmod p\n=\n\\sum_{u\\geq-1}P_u'M^0(u,k)\n-\\frac32\\sum_{u\\geq0}D_u'Z^0(u,k).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell\\geq H-2p\\), whenever the columns exist,\n\\[\nX_\\ell=V_\\ell,\\qquad X_{p+\\ell}=U_\\ell-V_\\ell.\n\\]\nAlso \\(U_\\ell=0\\) for \\(\\ell\\leq A\\).</p><p>If \\(2\\sqrt H&lt;p\\leq H/2\\), define\n\\[\nd_0=\\bigl(\\min(p,L-p,A)-\\max(b,H-2p)\\bigr)_+,\n\\qquad y_+=\\max(y,0).\n\\]\nThen every full raw minor, and hence \\(\\Delta_N\\), satisfies\n\\[\nv_p(\\Delta_N)\\geq-2n+(d_0-q)_+.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Gather Lemma 3.1 by coefficient residue. A term \\([t^i]P\\) has its unique residue \\(d\\) satisfying\n\\[\ni+1+d=(i'+1)p+\\ell,\n\\]\nand contributes to \\(P_{i'}'\\), including \\(i'=-1\\). The \\(Z^0\\)-reduction survives exactly at \\(i=up+\\ell\\), giving \\(D_u'\\). The coefficient of \\(G\\) in a raw column is a sum of integer multiples of \\(c_{(i-j)/2}\\), which are integral at every odd prime; since \\(G\\) is integral at \\(p\\), its contribution disappears after multiplication by \\(p^2\\). This gives the formula and \\(p^{-2}\\)-integrality.</p><p>If \\(\\ell\\geq H-2p\\), the bounds\n\\[\n\\deg(tPE)\\leq H+p-1,\\qquad \\deg D&lt;H\n\\]\nshow that \\(P_u',D_u'\\) vanish for \\(u&gt;1\\). The needed reduced values are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrr}\nu&amp;-1&amp;0&amp;1\\\\ \\hline\nM^0(u,0)&amp;0&amp;0&amp;2\\\\\nM^0(u,1)&amp;2&amp;0&amp;-2\n\\end{array}\n\\qquad\n\\begin{array}{c|rr}\nu&amp;0&amp;1\\\\ \\hline\nZ^0(u,0)&amp;0&amp;1\\\\\nZ^0(u,1)&amp;1&amp;-1\n\\end{array}.\n\\]\nThey yield \\(X_\\ell=V_\\ell\\) and \\(X_{p+\\ell}=U_\\ell-V_\\ell\\). Since \\(tP,D\\) have no terms below degree \\(A+1\\), \\(U_\\ell=0\\) for \\(\\ell\\leq A\\).</p><p>The transfer through column changes is as follows. Let \\(T\\) be the \\(n\\times(n+q)\\) raw matrix. If \\(Q\\in\\mathrm{GL}_{n+q}(\\mathbb Z_p)\\) and every full minor of \\(TQ\\) has valuation at least \\(-D_0\\), the same holds for \\(T=(TQ)Q^{-1}\\) by Cauchy–Binet, since minors of \\(Q^{-1}\\) are integral. A second Cauchy–Binet expansion transfers this to the prescribed integer filter. This remains valid if columns or leading residues are dependent.</p><p>For \\(p\\leq H/2\\), use each pair with\n\\[\n\\max(b,H-2p)\\leq\\ell&lt;\\min(p,L-p,A).\n\\]\nThere are \\(d_0\\) disjoint pairs. Replace the high column by its sum with the low one. The operation and inverse are integral. The sum belongs to \\(p^{-1}\\mathbb Z_p^n\\); all other columns remain in \\(p^{-2}\\mathbb Z_p^n\\). Any selection of \\(n\\) columns from \\(n+q\\) contains at least \\((d_0-q)_+\\) improved columns. The transfer proves the bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 3, and take an odd prime \\(p\\) in the range of Lemma 3.1 with \\(H/2&lt;p\\leq H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  K=L-p,\\qquad J=H-p.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A central column is one indexed by\n  \\[\n  b\\leq j&lt;\\min(p,L),\\qquad j\\geq J.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the vectors \\(V_\\ell\\) of Lemma 3.2.</p><p>Statement (paper, Lemma 3.2, “Central layer”).\nFor every central column,\n\\[\npF_j\\in\\mathbb Z_p^n,\\qquad\npF_j\\equiv-\\sum_{0\\leq\\ell&lt;J}\\frac{V_\\ell}{j-\\ell}\\pmod p.\n\\]\nEvery denominator \\(j-\\ell\\) in this expression is a unit at \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For every contributing degree \\(0\\leq i&lt;H\\),\n\\[\ni-j\\leq H-1-J=p-1,\\qquad j-i&lt;p.\n\\]\nThus \\(|i-j|&lt;p\\), and the starting boundary value in diagonal reduction is integral at \\(p\\). In either case the reduction can be written as that start minus\n\\[\n\\sum_{0\\leq\\ell&lt;\\min(i,j)}\\frac{m_{i-\\ell-1}}{j-\\ell}.\n\\]\nHere \\(1\\leq j-\\ell&lt;p\\). By the moment reduction in Lemma 3.1, a nonzero reduction after multiplication by \\(p\\) requires\n\\[\np\\leq i-\\ell&lt;2p,\n\\qquad\npm_{i-\\ell-1}\\equiv2E_{2p+\\ell-1-i}.\n\\]\nSuch an index has \\(0\\leq\\ell&lt;J\\). Conversely, its coefficient can be collected over all \\(i\\), since \\(j\\geq J&gt;\\ell\\) and \\(i\\geq p+\\ell&gt;\\ell\\). Therefore\n\\[\npM^0(P,j)\\equiv-\\sum_{0\\leq\\ell&lt;J}\\frac{2P_1'}{j-\\ell}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(Z^0(i,j)\\), harmonic sums have a pole only at \\(i=p+\\ell\\), \\(0\\leq\\ell&lt;J\\). The denominator \\(i-j\\) is a unit, since congruence to zero would force \\(j=\\ell&lt;J\\). Hence\n\\[\npZ^0(p+\\ell,j)\\equiv\\frac1{\\ell-j}.\n\\]\nAll other \\(Z^0(i,j)\\) are integral. Combining the calculations with \\(-3/2\\) gives the result. The \\(G\\)-term is integral as before.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Lemma 3.3, including \\(K=L-p\\) and \\(J=H-p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  R=K_++(K-A)_++(J-\\max(b,K))_+,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  S=(\\min(A,K)-b)_+\n  +(\\min(b,J)-\\max(0,K))_+.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Lemma 3.3, “Elimination over the local integers”).\nEvery full raw minor, and hence the filtered determinant, satisfies\n\\[\nv_p(\\Delta_N)\\geq-\\min(2n,n+R,2R+S).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The noncentral columns are precisely the high columns \\(F_{p+\\ell}\\), \\(0\\leq\\ell&lt;K\\), and the low columns \\(F_\\ell\\), \\(b\\leq\\ell&lt;J\\). Empty ranges are allowed: \\(p&gt;b\\), \\(J&lt;p\\), and \\(K&lt;J\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\nb\\leq\\ell&lt;\\min(A,K),\n\\]\nretain the low column and replace the high column by \\(F_{p+\\ell}+F_\\ell\\). The number of pair sums is\n\\[\nB=(\\min(A,K)-b)_+.\n\\]\nEach is in \\(p^{-1}\\mathbb Z_p^n\\). Retain every other noncentral column in \\(p^{-2}\\mathbb Z_p^n\\), whether or not its leading residue depends on the others. Their number is\n\\[\nR_0=K_++(J-b)_+-B.\n\\]\nThis equals \\(R\\). Splitting into \\(K\\leq0\\), \\(0&lt;K\\leq b\\), \\(b&lt;K\\leq A\\), and \\(K&gt;A\\), the respective formulas are\n\\[\n(J-b)_+,\\quad K+(J-b)_+,\\quad J,\\quad K+J-A.\n\\]</p><p>These retained columns represent every \\(V_\\ell\\), \\(0\\leq\\ell&lt;J\\), except possibly those indexed by\n\\[\n\\mathcal E=\\{\\ell:\\max(0,K)\\leq\\ell&lt;\\min(b,J)\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(b\\leq\\ell&lt;J\\), the retained low column has residue \\(p^2F_\\ell\\equiv V_\\ell\\). For \\(0\\leq\\ell&lt;\\min(b,K)\\), the unpaired high column has residue \\(-V_\\ell\\), since \\(\\ell&lt;b&lt;A\\) implies \\(U_\\ell=0\\). Thus\n\\[\ne:=|\\mathcal E|=(\\min(b,J)-\\max(0,K))_+.\n\\]</p><p>For each represented residue choose a retained column \\(Y_\\ell\\) and sign \\(\\sigma_\\ell\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\) such that\n\\[\np^2Y_\\ell\\equiv\\sigma_\\ell V_\\ell.\n\\]\nFor each central \\(j\\), choose \\(a_{\\ell j}\\in\\mathbb Z_p\\) reducing to \\(\\sigma_\\ell/(j-\\ell)\\), and replace the column by\n\\[\nC_j=F_j+p\\sum_{\\substack{0\\leq\\ell&lt;J\\\\\\ell\\notin\\mathcal E}}\na_{\\ell j}Y_\\ell.\n\\]\nThis is an integral shear with integral inverse. It preserves retained columns and pair sums. By Lemma 3.3,\n\\[\nC_j\\in p^{-1}\\mathbb Z_p^n,\\qquad\npC_j\\equiv-\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal E}\\frac{V_\\ell}{j-\\ell}\\pmod p.\n\\]\nNo next-coefficient information is required: if\n\\[\nY_\\ell=p^{-2}y_0+p^{-1}y_1+y_2\n\\]\nwith integral lifts, then \\(pY_\\ell=p^{-1}y_0+y_1+py_2\\), whose unknown second coefficient is already integral.</p><p>Let \\(c\\) be the number of central columns. Their scaled residues define\n\\[\n\\varphi:\\mathbb F_p^c\\longrightarrow\\mathbb F_p^n,\\qquad\n(a_j)_j\\longmapsto\\sum_ja_j(pC_j\\bmod p).\n\\]\nIts image lies in the span of the \\(e\\) exceptional vectors, so \\(d:=\\operatorname{rank}\\varphi\\leq\\min(c,e)\\). Choose a basis of \\(\\mathbb F_p^c\\) whose last \\(c-d\\) vectors span \\(\\ker\\varphi\\), and lift its matrix to \\(W\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_c(\\mathbb Z_p)\\). Its determinant is a unit; the adjugate formula gives an integral inverse. This change leaves at most \\(d\\) central columns in \\(p^{-1}\\mathbb Z_p^n\\); the others are integral. If \\(c=0\\), use the empty identity.</p><p>The pool now has \\(R\\) columns with possible denominator \\(p^2\\), at most\n\\[\nB+d\\leq B+e=S\n\\]\nother columns with possible denominator \\(p\\), and integral remaining columns. No noncentral column was discarded or improved solely from leading dependence.</p><p>In a full minor, with \\(r\\) columns from the first group and \\(s\\) from the second, the loss is at most \\(2r+s\\), and\n\\[\n2r+s\\leq2n,\\qquad\n2r+s\\leq n+R,\\qquad\n2r+s\\leq2R+S.\n\\]\nApply the Cauchy–Binet transfer of Lemma 3.2.</p><p>The optional pair at \\(\\ell=A\\) was omitted, but its column is included in \\(R\\). The proof covers \\(K\\leq0\\) and \\(J\\leq b\\). If \\(L\\leq p\\leq H\\), all raw columns are central, \\(R=0\\), and \\(S=H-p\\); at the formal endpoint \\(p=H\\), all are integral.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(G\\in\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\Delta_N\\) from Proposition 1 and \\(v_p(p)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An underlined row polynomial is formed with scale \\(N=1\\):\n  \\[\n  n_0=48,\\quad b_0=7,\\quad q_0=g_0=4,\\quad h_0=2,\\quad\n  L_0=59,\\ C_0=63,\\ H_0=65.\n  \\]\n  In addition to rows \\(0,\\ldots,47\\), define row \\(48\\) by the same row formulas.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\leq r\\leq48\\), \\(0\\leq k&lt;48\\), define the fixed rational matrix\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal B(r,k)=\n  \\sum_{j=0}^{4}(-1)^j\\binom4j\n  \\left(\n  M^0(\\underline P_r,7+k+j)\n  -\\frac32Z^0(\\underline D_r,7+k+j)\n  \\right).\n  \\]\n  Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal B_0=(\\mathcal B(r,k))_{0\\leq r,k&lt;48},\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal B_1=(\\mathcal B(r+1,k))_{0\\leq r,k&lt;48}.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Proposition 4.1).\nFor every sufficiently large prime \\(p\\),\n\\[\nv_p(\\Delta_p)=-96p.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\Delta_p\\ne0\\) for every sufficiently large prime \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal E\\) consist of \\(2\\), primes dividing the denominator of \\(G\\), primes dividing a denominator of an entry of \\(\\mathcal B\\), and primes dividing a numerator or denominator, in lowest terms, of any of the three nonzero determinants in Lemma 4.4. This is a fixed finite set.</p><p>For \\(p&gt;260\\) outside \\(\\mathcal E\\), the three fixed matrices reduce to invertible matrices over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). Lemmas 4.1 and 4.3 imply \\(\\det\\mathcal L_p\\ne0\\). Since \\(n=48p\\),\n\\[\np^{2n}\\Delta_p=\\det(p^2F_p^{\\mathrm{fil}})\n\\]\nis a unit in \\(\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\). Therefore\n\\[\nv_p(\\Delta_p)=-2n=-96p.\n\\]\nEvery sufficiently large prime exceeds \\(260\\) and lies outside \\(\\mathcal E\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be an odd prime and work over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal P_p=\n  \\{Q\\in\\mathbb F_p[w]:\n  \\deg Q\\leq2p-2,\\ w^{2p-2}Q(1/w)=Q(w)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\leq\\ell,i&lt;p\\), put\n  \\[\n  U_\\ell(w)=(2w)^\\ell(1+w^2)^{p-1-\\ell},\n  \\qquad\n  Q_i(w)=w^i\\sum_{j=0}^{p-i-1}w^{2j}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(a_{\\ell i}\\) by \\(Q_i=\\sum_\\ell a_{\\ell i}U_\\ell\\), and let \\(a=(a_{\\ell i})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(Q_0'=0\\), \\(Q_i'=Q_{p-i}\\) for \\(1\\leq i&lt;p\\), and write \\(Q_i'=\\sum_\\ell b_{\\ell i}U_\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Pi e_0=0,\\qquad \\Pi e_i=e_{p-i}\\quad(1\\leq i&lt;p),\n  \\]\n  so \\(b=a\\Pi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(t=2w/(1+w^2)\\), \\(f=(1-w^2)/(1+w^2)\\), and \\(E(t)=f^{p-1}\\).</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 4.1).\nBoth \\((U_\\ell)\\) and \\((Q_i)\\) are bases of the \\(p\\)-dimensional space \\(\\mathcal P_p\\), and\n\\[\na_{\\ell i}=0\\quad(\\ell&lt;i),\\qquad\na_{ii}=2^{-i},\\qquad\n\\det a=2^{-p(p-1)/2}\\ne0.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nE(t)w^i=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{p-1}t^\\ell(a_{\\ell i}+b_{\\ell i}w^p).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The coefficients of \\(w^0,\\ldots,w^{p-1}\\) determine a member of \\(\\mathcal P_p\\), so its dimension is \\(p\\). Both families belong to this space. Their lowest terms are \\(2^\\ell w^\\ell\\) and \\(w^i\\), respectively. Triangularity in degrees \\(0,\\ldots,p-1\\) proves the basis assertions and the formulas for \\(a\\).</p><p>The explicit forms are\n\\[\nQ_i=w^i\\frac{1-w^{2(p-i)}}{1-w^2},\n\\qquad\nQ_i'=w^{p-i}\\frac{1-w^{2i}}{1-w^2},\n\\]\nthe latter being zero also at \\(i=0\\). Hence\n\\[\nQ_i+w^pQ_i'\n=w^i\\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}w^{2j}\n=w^i(1-w^2)^{p-1},\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\binom{p-1}{j}=(-1)^j\\) in \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). Divide by \\((1+w^2)^{p-1}\\) to obtain the rational-function identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 4 with scale \\(N=p\\), where \\(p\\) is an odd prime.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(r=pr_0+i\\), \\(0\\leq r_0&lt;48\\), \\(0\\leq i&lt;p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(t'=t^p\\), \\(w'=w^p\\), \\(f'=f^p\\), and use the matrices \\(a,b\\) of Lemma 4.1.</p><p class=\"li\">For a fixed residue \\(\\ell\\), use the extractions\n  \\[\n  P_u'=[t^{(u+1)p+\\ell}]\\,tP_r(t)E(t),\n  \\qquad\n  D_u'=[t^{up+\\ell}]D_r(t).\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 4.2).\nOver \\(\\mathbb F_p[t]\\),\n\\[\ntP_r(t)E(t)\n=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{p-1}t^\\ell t'\n\\left(\na_{\\ell i}\\underline P_{r_0}(t')\n+b_{\\ell i}\\underline P_{r_0+1}(t')\n\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\nD_r(t)=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{p-1}t^\\ell\n\\left(\na_{\\ell i}\\underline D_{r_0}(t')\n+b_{\\ell i}\\underline D_{r_0+1}(t')\n\\right).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n(P_u',D_u')\n=[(t')^u]\\left\\{\na_{\\ell i}(\\underline P_{r_0},\\underline D_{r_0})(t')\n+b_{\\ell i}(\\underline P_{r_0+1},\\underline D_{r_0+1})(t')\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(P_{-1}'=0\\). The auxiliary row \\(48\\), including its lower-degree coefficients, is retained.</p>", "proof": "<p>Frobenius gives\n\\[\nt'=\\frac{2w'}{1+(w')^2},\\qquad\nf'=\\frac{1-(w')^2}{1+(w')^2},\\qquad\n(f')^2=1-(t')^2.\n\\]\nUsing \\(C=63p\\), \\(h=2p\\), \\(g=4p\\), and Lemma 4.1,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ntR_rE(t)\n&amp;=(1-t')^2(t')^{63}(w')^{r_0-4}E(t)w^i\\\\\n&amp;=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{p-1}t^\\ell t'\n\\left(\na_{\\ell i}\\underline R_{r_0}(t',w')\n+b_{\\ell i}\\underline R_{r_0+1}(t',w')\n\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe single \\(w'\\) in the second term creates exactly one successor row, of index at most \\(48\\).</p><p>The involution fixes \\(t,t'\\) and negates \\(f,f'\\). Since \\(fE=f'\\),\n\\[\ntR_rE=tP_rE-f'D_r,\\qquad\nt'\\underline R_j=t'\\underline P_j-f'\\underline D_j.\n\\]\nTaking invariant and anti-invariant parts, and cancelling the nonzero rational function \\(f'\\) in the latter, gives the two polynomial identities. Substitution \\(t=2w/(1+w^2)\\) is injective on \\(\\mathbb F_p[t]\\).</p><p>Every polynomial has a unique expression\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{p-1}t^\\ell A_\\ell(t^p).\n\\]\nThe extraction formula follows: the factor \\(t'\\) is consumed by \\(u+1\\) in the \\(P\\)-extraction, while the \\(D\\)-extraction has no shift. Negative coefficient indices are zero.</p><p>For the last successor row,\n\\[\n\\min\\deg\\underline P_{48}=62-44=18,\\qquad\n\\min\\deg\\underline D_{48}=63-44=19.\n\\]\nThese coefficients must be retained; the first \\(48\\) base rows have minimum degrees at least \\(19,20\\). For \\(r=47p+i\\), \\(i&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nb_{\\ell i}=a_{\\ell,p-i}=0\\quad(\\ell&lt;p-i),\n\\qquad b_{p-i,i}=2^{-(p-i)}\\ne0.\n\\]\nThus the successor contribution in either \\(tP_rE\\) or \\(D_r\\) starts at\n\\[\n19p+(p-i)=20p-i.\n\\]\nThe first-row contribution starts no earlier than \\(20p+i\\). Directly, \\(|r-g|=43p+i\\), so \\(tP_r,D_r\\) start at \\(20p-i\\), agreeing with \\(E(0)=1\\). The leading Chebyshev coefficients are powers of \\(2\\), nonzero in odd characteristic. For \\(i=0\\), the successor term vanishes and the first begins at \\(20p\\). There is therefore no forbidden coefficient. The auxiliary contact identity is valid since\n\\[\nt\\underline R_{48}/f=O(t^{107}),\\qquad107&gt;59.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 4 and take \\(p&gt;260\\), excluding primes dividing the denominator of \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_p^{\\mathrm{fil}}\\) denote the \\(48p\\times48p\\) filtered matrix whose determinant is \\(\\Delta_p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Order rows by \\((i,r_0)\\), and columns by \\((\\ell,k_0)\\), where the original filtered column index is \\(k=\\ell+pk_0\\), \\(0\\leq\\ell&lt;p\\), \\(0\\leq k_0&lt;48\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(a,b,\\Pi\\) from Lemma 4.1.</p><p>Statement (paper, Sections 4.3–4.4, equations (57)–(60)).\nThe matrix \\(p^2F_p^{\\mathrm{fil}}\\) is locally integral. Its reduction, in the stated ordering, is\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_p=a^T\\otimes\\mathcal B_0+b^T\\otimes\\mathcal B_1,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_p\\bigl((a^T)^{-1}\\otimes I_{48}\\bigr)\n=I_p\\otimes\\mathcal B_0+\\Pi^T\\otimes\\mathcal B_1.\n\\]\nIts determinant is\n\\[\n\\det\\mathcal L_p\n=(\\det a)^{48}\\det\\mathcal B_0\\,\n\\det(\\mathcal B_0+\\mathcal B_1)^{(p-1)/2}\n\\det(\\mathcal B_0-\\mathcal B_1)^{(p-1)/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Here \\(H=65p\\), and \\(p&gt;260\\) implies \\(p&gt;2\\sqrt{65p}\\), so the digit and extraction reductions in Proposition 3 apply. The rational raw entry is\n\\[\nF_j(r)=M^0(P_r,j)-\\frac32Z^0(D_r,j)\n+4G\\sum_v[t^v]P_rc_{(v-j)/2}.\n\\]\nThe last sum is integral at an odd prime. The \\(G\\)-term therefore disappears after multiplication by \\(p^2\\); the scaled entries are integral.</p><p>Substituting Lemma 4.2 into the extraction formula yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np^2F_{kp+\\ell}(pr_0+i)\\equiv&amp;\na_{\\ell i}\\left(\nM^0(\\underline P_{r_0},k)-\\frac32Z^0(\\underline D_{r_0},k)\n\\right)\\\\\n&amp;+b_{\\ell i}\\left(\nM^0(\\underline P_{r_0+1},k)-\\frac32Z^0(\\underline D_{r_0+1},k)\n\\right)\\pmod p.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe base entries have denominator prime factors at most \\(65\\), as the recipes in Lemma 4.4 show.</p><p>The integer filter respects residues because\n\\[\ns^{7p+k}(1-s)^{4p}\n=s^\\ell(s^p)^{7+k_0}(1-s^p)^4\n\\quad\\text{in }\\mathbb F_p[s].\n\\]\nAfter scaling the entries by \\(p^2\\), coefficient differences divisible by \\(p\\) multiply integral entries. The fourth difference on the right uses exactly the five base indices \\(7+k_0,\\ldots,11+k_0\\), all in \\(7,\\ldots,58\\). Each residue group therefore contains \\(48\\) base filtered columns, without changing \\(\\ell\\). Its \\((i,\\ell)\\)-block is\n\\[\na_{\\ell i}\\mathcal B_0+b_{\\ell i}\\mathcal B_1.\n\\]\nThis proves the Kronecker expression. Since \\(b^T=\\Pi^Ta^T\\), multiplication on the stated side gives the second identity.</p><p>The indices \\(1,\\ldots,p-1\\) form \\((p-1)/2\\) pairs \\(\\{i,p-i\\}\\). Their sum and difference vectors are eigenvectors of \\(\\Pi\\) with eigenvalues \\(1,-1\\). Together with \\(e_0\\), of eigenvalue zero, they form a basis because \\(2\\) is invertible. A similarity on the \\(p\\)-dimensional factor gives the determinant formula; the similarity adds no determinant factor. Returning to the original orders changes at most the sign.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed rational matrices \\(\\mathcal B_0,\\mathcal B_1\\) of Proposition 4.</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 4.4, equation (48) and Table 1).\nThe three rational matrices satisfy\n\\[\n\\det\\mathcal B_0\\ne0,\\qquad\n\\det(\\mathcal B_0+\\mathcal B_1)\\ne0,\\qquad\n\\det(\\mathcal B_0-\\mathcal B_1)\\ne0.\n\\]\nTheir nonvanishing is certified by exact Gaussian elimination modulo \\(101\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Construct the entries rationally as follows. Form \\(m_0=2\\), \\(m_i=0\\) for odd \\(i\\), and\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{i\\,m_{i-2}}{i+1}\\qquad(2\\leq i\\leq64,\\ i\\text{ even}).\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nk_0^-=0,\\quad k_1^-=2,\\quad k_0^+=k_1^+=0,\n\\]\nand use\n\\[\nk_d^-=\\frac{(d-1)k_{d-2}^-+m_{d-2}+m_{d-1}}d\n\\qquad(2\\leq d\\leq64),\n\\]\n\\[\nk_d^+=\\frac{(d-2)k_{d-2}^++2/(d-1)}{d-1}\n\\qquad(2\\leq d\\leq58).\n\\]\nOn \\(0\\leq i\\leq64\\), \\(0\\leq j\\leq58\\), form\n\\[\nY_{i0}=k_i^-,\\qquad Y_{0j}=k_j^+,\\qquad\nY_{ij}=Y_{i-1,j-1}-\\frac{m_{i-1}}j\\quad(i,j\\geq1).\n\\]\nThus \\(Y_{ij}=M^0(i,j)\\). Using \\(B_i^{(d)}=\\sum_{u=1}^iu^{-d}\\), put\n\\[\nJ_{ij}=\\frac32\n\\begin{cases}\n-B_i^{(2)},&amp;i=j,\\\\\n(B_i^{(1)}-B_j^{(1)})/(i-j),&amp;i\\ne j.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThus \\(J_{ij}=\\frac32Z^0(i,j)\\), with the diagonal sign included.</p><p>Define integer polynomials\n\\[\nE_0=t^{62},\\quad E_1=t^{61},\\quad I_0=0,\\quad I_1=t^{62},\n\\]\nand, for either family \\(S=E,I\\),\n\\[\nS_m=2S_{m-1}/t-S_{m-2}\\qquad(2\\leq m\\leq44).\n\\]\nThey are \\(E_m=t^{62}T_m(1/t)\\), \\(I_m=t^{62}U_{m-1}(1/t)\\). For \\(0\\leq r\\leq48\\), set \\(u=|r-4|\\) and\n\\[\ny_r=(1-t)^2E_u,\\qquad\nz_r=\\operatorname{sign}(r-4)(1-t)^2I_u.\n\\]\nThese are precisely \\(\\underline P_r,\\underline D_r\\). Contract their coefficients in degrees \\(0,\\ldots,64\\):\n\\[\nv_r(j)=\\sum_{i=0}^{64}\n\\bigl([t^i]y_rY_{ij}-[t^i]z_rJ_{ij}\\bigr),\n\\qquad j=7,\\ldots,58.\n\\]\nReplace this length-\\(52\\) vector four times by consecutive differences \\(v(j)-v(j+1)\\). The resulting length-\\(48\\) vector is row \\(r\\) of \\(\\mathcal B\\), including the auxiliary degree-\\(18\\) coefficient.</p><p>Every scalar denominator is a product of nonzero integers of absolute value at most \\(65\\). Hence every denominator is a unit modulo \\(101\\). Division by \\(t\\) merely shifts polynomial exponents. The whole construction can therefore be performed in \\(\\mathbb F_{101}\\) and agrees with rational reduction.</p><p>For \\(\\sigma\\in\\{0,1,-1\\}\\), begin with rows\n\\[\nR_i=\\mathcal B(i,\\cdot)+\\sigma\\mathcal B(i+1,\\cdot),\n\\qquad0\\leq i&lt;48.\n\\]\nAt step \\(i\\), if \\(R_i(i)=0\\), swap with the first later row having a nonzero entry in column \\(i\\). Record \\(d_i=R_i(i)\\), and replace each \\(R_j\\), \\(j&gt;i\\), by\n\\[\nR_j-\\frac{R_j(i)}{d_i}R_i.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\sigma=0,-1\\), there are no swaps. For \\(\\sigma=1\\), the only swaps are \\((30,31)\\), \\((45,46)\\), with indices starting at zero. The exact pivots, consecutively in groups of sixteen, are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|l}\n\\sigma&amp;\\text{successive pivots in }\\mathbb F_{101}\\\\ \\hline\n0&amp;\n60,68,62,79,47,32,69,57,30,72,35,66,43,20,85,48\\\\\n&amp;\n88,4,77,54,60,79,26,68,83,39,40,65,1,68,78,24\\\\\n&amp;\n15,98,32,22,94,9,99,10,15,75,4,2,25,53,90,79\\\\ \\hline\n1&amp;\n38,51,90,70,5,21,88,55,45,20,35,41,77,10,18,25\\\\\n&amp;\n76,14,38,72,6,66,56,35,83,58,56,11,17,20,30,24\\\\\n&amp;\n28,11,25,70,79,99,66,38,4,41,63,91,63,17,90,98\\\\ \\hline\n-1&amp;\n82,92,26,87,21,74,87,88,88,3,14,23,38,58,36,20\\\\\n&amp;\n26,33,94,74,78,45,93,86,73,66,45,30,61,3,88,27\\\\\n&amp;\n20,58,69,48,78,39,48,1,66,18,86,93,52,92,39,77\n\\end{array}.\n\\]\nEvery pivot is nonzero. The swaps and row additions are invertible, so all three matrices are invertible modulo \\(101\\), and hence have nonzero rational determinants.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 1, without assuming \\(G\\in\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  d\\mu(t)=\\frac{|t|}{f(t)}\\,dt,\\qquad\n  A_r(t)=P_r(t)-\\frac32\\mathbf1_{\\{t&gt;0\\}}\\frac{f(t)}tD_r(t),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\psi_k(s)=s^{b+k}(1-s)^q.\n  \\]\n  The value at \\(t=0\\) is immaterial; \\(D_r(t)/t\\) is a polynomial.</p><p class=\"li\">For a list \\(y=(y_1,\\ldots,y_n)\\), write\n  \\[\n  V(y)=\\prod_{i&lt;j}(y_j-y_i),\\qquad \\mathcal V(y)=|V(y)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  x_i=\\frac{t_i}{1+\\sqrt{1-t_i^2}},\\qquad\n  t_i=\\frac{2x_i}{1+x_i^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For nonzero \\(x\\in(-1,1)\\), define\n  \\[\n  (\\xi_{\\mathrm f}(x),\\xi_{\\mathrm n}(x))=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  (1/2,1/2),&amp;x&lt;0,\\\\\n  (-1/4,5/4),&amp;x&gt;0,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal R_n(x)=\n  \\det_{r,i}\\left[\n  \\xi_{\\mathrm f}(x_i)x_i^{g-r}\n  +\\xi_{\\mathrm n}(x_i)x_i^{r-g}\n  \\right].\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 5.1, equations (62)–(65)).\nThe determinant has the absolutely convergent representations\n\\[\n\\Delta_N=\\frac1{(n!)^2}\n\\int_{(-1,1)^n}\\int_{(0,1)^n}\n\\det[A_r(t_i)]_{r,i}\n\\det\\left[\\frac1{1-t_is_j}\\right]_{i,j}\n\\det[\\psi_k(s_j)]_{j,k}\\,ds\\,d\\mu^n(t),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Delta_N=\\frac1{(n!)^2}\\iint\n\\mathcal R_n(x)\\,\n\\frac{V(t)V(s)^2}{\\prod_{i,j}(1-t_is_j)}\n\\prod_js_j^b(1-s_j)^q\n\\prod_it_i^{C-1}(1-t_i)^h\\,ds\\,d\\mu^n(t).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nd\\mu(t_i)=\\frac{4|x_i|}{(1+x_i^2)^2}\\,dx_i,\n\\qquad \\mu((-1,1))=2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The entries defining \\(\\Delta_N\\) are exactly\n\\[\n\\int_{-1}^1\\int_0^1\nA_r(t)\\frac{\\psi_k(s)}{1-ts}\\,ds\\,d\\mu(t).\n\\]\nThe scalar majorant\n\\[\n\\iint(1-|t|s)^{-1}\\,ds\\,d\\mu(t)\n\\]\nis finite: integration in \\(s\\) gives at worst a logarithmic singularity at \\(|t|=1\\), integrable against \\((1-t^2)^{-1/2}dt\\). For fixed \\(n\\), all \\(A_r,\\psi_k\\) are bounded. All permutation expansions are therefore absolutely integrable.</p><p>Apply Andréief’s identity twice. Expanding the two determinants sharing an integration list shows directly that its \\(n!\\) permutations give the same determinant of single integrals. This yields one factor \\(1/n!\\) at each application. Fubini applies by the preceding majorant.</p><p>Cauchy’s multiplicative double alternant gives\n\\[\n\\det\\left[\\frac1{1-t_is_j}\\right]_{i,j}\n=\\frac{V(t)V(s)}{\\prod_{i,j}(1-t_is_j)}.\n\\]\nTo verify the identity, multiply by the denominator. The resulting polynomial alternates separately in \\(t,s\\), and has degree at most \\(n-1\\) in each variable. Dividing by \\(V(t)V(s)\\) leaves a constant. The first nonzero homogeneous part of the power-series expansion at \\(s=0\\) is \\(V(t)V(s)\\), so the constant is one. Also\n\\[\n\\det[\\psi_k(s_j)]_{j,k}\n=V(s)\\prod_js_j^b(1-s_j)^q.\n\\]</p><p>The coordinate change maps \\((-1,1)\\) bijectively onto itself and gives the stated density. Away from a null set, the \\(x_i\\) are distinct and nonzero. Since\n\\[\nfD_r/t=(R_r^*-R_r)/2,\n\\]\non the negative half-interval the row is \\((R_r+R_r^*)/2\\), and on the positive half-interval it is \\((5R_r-R_r^*)/4\\). Factor \\((1-t_i)^ht_i^{C-1}\\) from column \\(i\\) to obtain the second representation. There is one \\(t\\)-Vandermonde and two \\(s\\)-Vandermondes; all denominators are positive.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal R_n\\), \\(g=4N\\), and \\(n=48N\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  D_*=n-1-2g=40N-1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\) be distinct nonzero real numbers in \\((-1,1)\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{i=1}^n\\frac{1-x_i^2}{1+x_i^2}\\leq D_*.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(K_0=10e^{12}\\).</p><p>Statement (paper, Proposition 5.2, “Interpolation estimate”).\nUniformly in the locations and separations of the nodes,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R_n(x)|\n\\leq(1+K_0n)^n\\mathcal V(1/x)\\prod_i|x_i|^g.\n\\]\nThe prefactor is \\(\\exp(O(n\\log n))\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Factor a row evaluation as\n\\[\n\\xi_{\\mathrm f}(x_i)x_i^{g-r}\n+\\xi_{\\mathrm n}(x_i)x_i^{r-g}\n=\n\\xi_{\\mathrm f}(x_i)x_i^{g-(n-1)}\n\\left(x_i^{n-1-r}+c_ix_i^{D_*}x_i^r\\right).\n\\]\nUse the interpolant from Lemma 6.1.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nQ(z)=\\prod_i(1-x_iz),\\qquad\n\\mathcal H_Q=\\{v/Q:v\\in\\mathbb C[z],\\ \\deg v&lt;n\\},\n\\]\nwith the norm inherited from \\(H^2\\):\n\\[\n\\|v/Q\\|^2=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\n\\frac{|v(e^{i\\theta})|^2}{|Q(e^{i\\theta})|^2}\\,d\\theta.\n\\]\nAll zeros of \\(Q\\) lie outside the closed disk.</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\widetilde v(z)=z^{n-1}v(1/z),\\qquad\nR(v/Q)=\\widetilde v/Q.\n\\]\nThis is a complex-linear isometric involution, since\n\\[\n|\\widetilde v(e^{i\\theta})|=|v(e^{-i\\theta})|,\n\\qquad\n|Q(e^{i\\theta})|=|Q(e^{-i\\theta})|.\n\\]\nThe kernels \\(k_i(z)=(1-x_iz)^{-1}\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal H_Q\\), are independent by their distinct poles, and form a basis. The \\(H^2\\) inner product gives\n\\[\n\\langle u,k_i\\rangle=u(x_i).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Pi_Q\\) is orthogonal projection onto \\(\\mathcal H_Q\\), then\n\\[\n(\\Pi_Qu)(x_i)=u(x_i).\n\\]</p><p>Multiplication by \\(h_*\\) has operator norm at most \\(\\|h_*\\|_\\infty\\). Thus\n\\[\nJ=\\Pi_QM_{h_*}|_{\\mathcal H_Q},\\qquad L=I+JR,\n\\qquad \\|L\\|\\leq1+K_0n.\n\\]\nLet \\(E:\\mathcal H_Q\\to\\mathbb C^n\\) send \\(v/Q\\) to \\((v(x_i))_i\\). It is invertible because a polynomial of degree less than \\(n\\) cannot vanish at all nodes. Also \\(E(u)_i=Q(x_i)u(x_i)\\). Since \\(LR=R+J\\), projection preserves values, and the \\(Q(x_i)\\) factors cancel algebraically,\n\\[\nELR(v/Q)=\\bigl(\\widetilde v(x_i)+h_*(x_i)v(x_i)\\bigr)_i.\n\\]\nIn the basis \\(1/Q,z/Q,\\ldots,z^{n-1}/Q\\), \\(\\det E=V(x)\\), while \\(|\\det R|=1\\). Hence the mixed evaluation determinant divided by \\(V(x)\\) has absolute value\n\\[\n|\\det L|\\leq\\|L\\|^n\\leq(1+K_0n)^n.\n\\]\nNo estimate for the inverse evaluation matrix is involved.</p><p>Use \\(|\\xi_{\\mathrm f}(x_i)|\\leq1/2\\leq1\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(1/x)=\\mathcal V(x)\\prod_i|x_i|^{-(n-1)}.\n\\]\nThe desired inequality follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the nodes, \\(D_*\\), and inequality of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  c_i=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  1,&amp;x_i&lt;0,\\\\\n  -5,&amp;x_i&gt;0.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Lemma 5.1).\nThere is a function \\(h_*\\), holomorphic on a neighborhood of the closed unit disk, such that\n\\[\nh_*(x_i)=c_ix_i^{D_*},\n\\qquad\n\\sup_{|z|\\leq1}|h_*(z)|\\leq K_0n.\n\\]\nThe neighborhood may depend on the node list; the displayed bound does not.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nB(z)=\\prod_i\\frac{z-x_i}{1-x_iz},\\qquad F(z)=\\frac{z^{D_*}}{B(z)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;u\\leq1\\) and \\(0&lt;|x|&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{d\\log u}\\frac12\\log\\frac{u^2+x^2}{1+x^2u^2}\n=\n\\frac{(1-x^4)u^2}{(u^2+x^2)(1+x^2u^2)}\n\\leq\\frac{1-x^2}{1+x^2}.\n\\]\nDivide the denominator by \\(u^2\\) and use \\(u^2+u^{-2}\\geq2\\). Integrating from \\(u\\) to \\(1\\) gives\n\\[\n|B(iu)|\\geq\nu^{\\sum_i(1-x_i^2)/(1+x_i^2)}\n\\geq u^{D_*}.\n\\]\nThe same holds at \\(-iu\\), and \\(F(0)=0\\). For \\(1\\leq u\\leq1+2/n\\), each factor of \\(B(iu)\\) has modulus at least one, since\n\\[\n(u^2+x^2)-(1+x^2u^2)=(u^2-1)(1-x^2)\\geq0.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n|F(iu)|\\leq e^2\\qquad(|u|\\leq1+2/n).\n\\]</p><p>Orient the segment\n\\[\n\\Gamma=[-i(1+2/n),i(1+2/n)]\n\\]\nupwards, and, off it, define\n\\[\n\\mathcal C(z)=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{6F(w)}{w-z}\\,dw.\n\\]\nWrite \\(c_-=1,c_+=-5\\), referring to the left and right half-planes, and put\n\\[\nh_*(z)=c_\\pm z^{D_*}-B(z)\\mathcal C(z)\n\\quad\\text{when }\\pm\\operatorname{Re}z&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The density is holomorphic near every point of \\(\\Gamma\\), because its poles \\(x_i\\) are real and nonzero. Local contour deformation and the Cauchy formula show that the left lateral value of \\(\\mathcal C\\) minus the right value is \\(6F\\). Moving an upward piece to its left gives a positively oriented closed contour and residue \\(6F(z)\\). The two continuations of \\(h_*\\) therefore differ by\n\\[\n(c_--c_+)z^{D_*}-6B(z)F(z)=0.\n\\]\nThis glues holomorphically across the segment, including its crossings of the unit circle.</p><p>For a fixed list, the poles \\(1/x_i\\) of \\(B\\) and endpoints of \\(\\Gamma\\) lie outside the closed disk. A sufficiently small exterior neighborhood avoids them, and the same gluing applies there. Since \\(B(x_i)=0\\) and \\(\\mathcal C\\) is regular at each \\(x_i\\), interpolation follows.</p><p>In a radius-\\(1/4\\) disk about \\(i\\) or \\(-i\\), the quantities \\(|w|\\), \\(|w-x_i|\\), and \\(|1-x_iw|\\) are at least \\(3/4\\). Thus \\(F\\) is nonzero there and\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{F'(w)}{F(w)}\\right|\n\\leq\\frac43D_*+\\frac83n&lt;4n.\n\\]\nSince \\(|F(i)|=|F(-i)|=1\\), integration on a segment gives\n\\[\n|F(w)|\\leq e^{12}\n\\]\nwhen \\(|w-i|\\leq3/n\\) or \\(|w+i|\\leq3/n\\).</p><p>If \\(|z|=1\\) and \\(|\\operatorname{Re}z|\\geq1/(10n)\\), the original segment is at distance at least \\(1/(10n)\\), has length at most \\(3\\), and\n\\[\n|\\mathcal C(z)|\\leq30e^2n.\n\\]\nIf \\(0&lt;|\\operatorname{Re}z|&lt;1/(10n)\\), \\(z\\) is near \\(i\\) or \\(-i\\). Near \\(i\\), replace the portion from \\(i(1-1/n)\\) to \\(i(1+2/n)\\) by the other three sides of the rectangle whose other vertical side has real part \\(-\\operatorname{sign}(\\operatorname{Re}z)/n\\). Near \\(-i\\), use imaginary endpoints \\(-1-2/n,-1+1/n\\). The swept rectangle lies opposite \\(z\\), contains neither \\(z\\) nor a pole of \\(F\\), and lies in the relevant radius-\\(1/4\\) disk, so the fixed integral is unchanged.</p><p>Every new side lies within \\(3/n\\) of the endpoint. The contour has length at most \\(3\\) and distance at least \\(1/(2n)\\) from \\(z\\): the vertical side has horizontal separation at least \\(1/n\\), the outer horizontal side has vertical separation at least \\(2/n\\), and the inner side and remaining diameter have separation at least\n\\[\n\\frac1n-\\left(1-\\sqrt{1-(10n)^{-2}}\\right)&gt;\\frac1{2n}.\n\\]\nHence \\(|\\mathcal C(z)|\\leq6e^{12}n\\). Only the endpoint neighborhoods were deformed; the middle diameter uses the bound \\(e^2\\), with no derivative estimate near zero.</p><p>Finally \\(|B(z)|=1\\) on \\(|z|=1\\), so \\(|h_*(z)|\\leq K_0n\\). At \\(z=\\pm i\\), use continuity. The maximum modulus principle gives the bound in the disk. No uniform exterior-neighborhood size was required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(n=48N\\), \\(g=4N\\), \\(D_*=n-1-2g\\), and \\(\\mathcal R_n\\) from Propositions 5–6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\) be any distinct nonzero real nodes in \\((-1,1)\\); the inequality in Proposition 6 is not required.</p><p>Statement (paper, Proposition 5.3, “Hadamard estimate”).\nFor every such list,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R_n(x)|\n\\leq\n(3/2)^n n^{n/2}2^{-\\binom n2}\n\\prod_i(1+x_i^2)^{(n-1)/2}|x_i|^{g-(n-1)}.\n\\]\nThe factor \\((3/2)^nn^{n/2}\\) is \\(\\exp(O(n\\log n))\\); the power \\(2^{-\\binom n2}\\) is retained in the principal integrand.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand the determinant by choosing a sheet in each column. For \\(z_i\\in\\{x_i,1/x_i\\}\\), the absolute monomial determinant is\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(z)\\prod_i|z_i|^{-g}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(z_i=\\tan\\varphi_i\\), \\(-\\pi/2&lt;\\varphi_i&lt;\\pi/2\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(z)=\n\\prod_i(1+z_i^2)^{(n-1)/2}\n\\prod_{i&lt;j}|\\sin(\\varphi_j-\\varphi_i)|.\n\\]\nThe last product is \\(2^{-\\binom n2}\\) times the Vandermonde on the unit-circle nodes \\(e^{2i\\varphi_i}\\). Its monomial matrix has column norms \\(\\sqrt n\\); Hadamard gives\n\\[\n\\prod_{i&lt;j}|\\sin(\\varphi_j-\\varphi_i)|\n\\leq2^{-\\binom n2}n^{n/2}.\n\\]</p><p>The ratio of the remaining far-sheet weight to the near-sheet weight is\n\\[\n\\frac{|1/x|^{-g}(1+x^{-2})^{(n-1)/2}}\n{|x|^{-g}(1+x^2)^{(n-1)/2}}\n=|x|^{-D_*}\\geq1.\n\\]\nBound every weight by its far-sheet value. The sum of absolute sheet coefficients is at most\n\\[\n\\prod_i\\bigl(|\\xi_{\\mathrm f}(x_i)|+|\\xi_{\\mathrm n}(x_i)|\\bigr)\n\\leq(3/2)^n.\n\\]\nThis proves the result.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the determinants of Proposition 1 without any rationality assumption, and the coordinates of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=\\frac{11}{48},\\qquad\n  \\beta=\\frac7{48},\\qquad\n  \\gamma=\\frac4{48},\\qquad\n  \\eta=\\frac2{48}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal J_n(x,s)=\n  \\frac{\\mathcal V(t)\\mathcal V(s)^2}{\\prod_{i,j}(1-t_is_j)}\n  \\prod_js_j^b(1-s_j)^q\n  \\prod_i|t_i|^{C-1}(1-t_i)^h,\n  \\quad t_i=\\frac{2x_i}{1+x_i^2},\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal I_{2,n}=\\mathcal J_n\\mathcal V(1/x)\\prod_i|x_i|^g,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal I_{1,n}=\n  \\mathcal J_n2^{-\\binom n2}\n  \\prod_i(1+x_i^2)^{(n-1)/2}|x_i|^{g-(n-1)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Case \\(\\kappa=2\\) consists of distinct nonzero \\(x_i\\in(-1,1)\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\sum_i\\frac{1-x_i^2}{1+x_i^2}\\leq D_*.\n  \\]\n  Case \\(\\kappa=1\\) consists of the complementary interior configurations. Always \\(0&lt;s_j&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  W_\\kappa(x)=\n  (\\alpha+2\\gamma)\\log|x|+2\\eta\\log(1-x)\n  -(\\kappa/2+\\alpha+\\eta+\\gamma)\\log(1+x^2),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  W_s(s)=\\beta\\log s+\\gamma\\log(1-s),\\qquad\n  D(x)=2\\gamma-\\frac{2x^2}{1+x^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a real exponentially decaying sequence \\(u=(u_k)_{k\\geq1}\\), set\n  \\[\n  \\|u\\|_*^2=\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{u_k^2}{k},\\qquad\n  T(u,x)=\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{u_kT_k(x)}k,\\qquad\n  S(u,x)=\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{u_kx^k}k.\n  \\]\n  Exponential decay means \\(|u_k|\\leq Kr^k\\) for some \\(K&lt;\\infty\\), \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p=(p_k)\\), \\(v=(v_k)\\) be two such sequences. In case \\(2\\), take \\(\\lambda=0\\); in case \\(1\\), take any fixed \\(\\lambda\\geq0\\). Here \\(p\\) denotes a sequence, not a prime.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\log0=-\\infty\\).</p><p>Statement (paper, Proposition 6.1).\nUniformly over the indicated case,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\\sup_{\\text{case }\\kappa}\n\\left(\\frac{\\log\\mathcal I_{\\kappa,n}}{n^2}-\\frac12\\log2\\right)\n\\leq{}&amp;\n(-1+\\alpha+\\gamma)\\log2\n+\\kappa\\|p\\|_*^2+\\frac12\\|v\\|_*^2\\\\\n&amp;+\\sup_{\\substack{-1\\leq x&lt;1\\\\x\\ne0}}\n\\{W_\\kappa(x)+\\lambda D(x)-2\\kappa T(p,x)-S(v,x)\\}\\\\\n&amp;+\\sup_{0&lt;s&lt;1}\\{W_s(s)+2T(v,s)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe maximum of these right sides for the two cases bounds\n\\[\n\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\n\\left(n^{-2}\\log|\\Delta_N|-\\frac12\\log2\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply\n\\[\n-a^2\\leq b^2-2ab\n\\]\nto the first square in Lemma 8.2 with \\(b=p_k\\), and to the second with \\(b=v_k\\). Dividing by \\(k\\) and summing gives\n\\[\n-\\kappa\\sum_kA_k^2/k\n\\leq\\kappa\\|p\\|_*^2-2\\kappa\\sum_kp_kA_k/k,\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac12\\sum_k(B_k-2C_k)^2/k\n\\leq\\frac12\\|v\\|_*^2-\\sum_kv_kB_k/k+2\\sum_kv_kC_k/k.\n\\]</p><p>In case \\(1\\), failure of the interpolation condition says\n\\[\n1-2\\left\\langle\\frac{x^2}{1+x^2}\\right\\rangle\n&gt;1-\\frac1n-2\\gamma,\n\\]\nso \\(\\langle D(x)\\rangle&gt;-1/n\\). Adding \\(\\lambda\\langle D(x)\\rangle\\) therefore costs at most \\(\\lambda/n\\) for an upper bound, explaining \\(\\lambda\\geq0\\) and its sign.</p><p>Let \\(T_\\varepsilon(p,x)\\), \\(S_\\varepsilon(v,x)\\), \\(T_\\varepsilon(v,s)\\) denote the series defining \\(T,S,T\\), with factors \\(\\tau^k,\\rho(x)^k,\\sigma(s)^k\\), respectively. Lemmas 8.1–8.2 reduce the bound to two one-variable suprema of\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{n,\\varepsilon}(x)={}&amp;\n\\frac{19}{48}\\log|x|\n+\\left(\\frac1{12}-\\frac1{2n}\\right)\\log(1-x)\\\\\n&amp;-(\\kappa/2+\\alpha+\\eta+\\gamma)\\log(1+x^2)\n+\\lambda D(x)-2\\kappa T_\\varepsilon(p,x)-S_\\varepsilon(v,x),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nY_{n,\\varepsilon}(s)=\n\\frac7{48}\\log s+\n\\left(\\frac1{12}-\\frac1n\\right)\\log(1-s)\n+2T_\\varepsilon(v,s),\n\\]\nplus the claimed constant and \\(O(\\varepsilon)+O_\\varepsilon(1/n)\\). All bounded finite-size corrections are in this error. For \\(n\\geq24\\), both weakened endpoint coefficients are at least \\(1/24\\).</p><p>Put \\(P_1=\\sum_k|p_k|\\), \\(V_1=\\sum_k|v_k|\\). These are finite. Since \\(1-(1-d)^k\\leq kd\\),\n\\[\n|T_\\varepsilon(p,x)-T(p,x)|\\leq\\varepsilon P_1,\n\\]\n\\[\n|S_\\varepsilon(v,x)-S(v,x)|\\leq2\\varepsilon V_1,\n\\qquad\n|T_\\varepsilon(v,s)-T(v,s)|\\leq\\varepsilon V_1,\n\\]\nuniformly. The changes in the tangent functions are thus at most\n\\[\n2\\kappa\\varepsilon P_1+2\\varepsilon V_1,\\qquad2\\varepsilon V_1.\n\\]\nNo uniform convergence of the raw square series is required.</p><p>The other nonsingular terms are uniformly bounded for \\(n\\geq24\\), \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/8\\). Positive coefficients \\(19/48,7/48\\), and the weakened coefficients at least \\(1/24\\), force uniform decay to \\(-\\infty\\) near \\(x=0,x=1,s=0,s=1\\). Values at \\(x=-1/2,s=1/2\\) have a common finite lower bound. Include the regular endpoint \\(x=-1\\) by continuity. Compact sets independent of \\(n\\) and small \\(\\varepsilon\\) therefore contain the maximizers.</p><p>On these sets, first let \\(n\\to\\infty\\) with \\(\\varepsilon\\) fixed, removing \\(O_\\varepsilon(1/n)\\), and then let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\). Uniform convergence gives the asserted bound. Finally use Lemma 8.1 to bound the determinant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">For averages write\n  \\[\n  \\langle F(x)\\rangle=\\frac1n\\sum_iF(x_i).\n  \\]\n  A double average \\(\\langle K(x,x')\\rangle_{\\ne}\\) sums over ordered pairs \\(i\\ne j\\) and divides by \\(n^2\\); without the subscript all pairs are included.</p><p>Statement (paper, Sections 5.4 and 6, equations (75), (79)–(80)).\nUniformly in the integration nodes,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log|\\Delta_N|}{n^2}-\\frac12\\log2\n\\leq\n\\max_{\\kappa=1,2}\\sup_{\\text{case }\\kappa}\n\\left(\\frac{\\log\\mathcal I_{\\kappa,n}}{n^2}-\\frac12\\log2\\right)\n+O\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log(n+2)}n\\right).\n\\]\nFor interior configurations,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\log\\mathcal I_{\\kappa,n}}{n^2}-\\frac12\\log2\n={}&amp;c_{\\kappa,n}\\log2+\n\\left\\langle W_\\kappa(x)+\\frac{\\kappa+2}{2n}\\log(1+x^2)\\right\\rangle\n+\\langle W_s(s)\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;+\\frac\\kappa2\\langle\\log|x-x'|\\rangle_{\\ne}\n+\\frac12\\langle\\log(1-xx')\\rangle_{\\ne}\\\\\n&amp;+\\langle\\log|s-s'|\\rangle_{\\ne}\n-\\langle\\log(1-2xs+x^2)\\rangle,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nc_{\\kappa,n}=\\frac Cn+\\frac{\\kappa-2}{2}-\\frac{\\kappa+1}{2n}.\n\\]\nThe displayed \\(\\log(1+x^2)\\) correction is bounded by \\(2\\log2/n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 5–7 give\n\\[\n|\\Delta_N|\\leq\n\\frac{2^nK_n}{(n!)^2}\n\\max_{\\kappa=1,2}\\sup_{\\text{case }\\kappa}\\mathcal I_{\\kappa,n},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nK_n=\\max\\{(1+K_0n)^n,(3/2)^nn^{n/2}\\}.\n\\]\nThe measure \\(d\\mu^n(t)\\,ds\\) has mass \\(2^n\\), and the cases partition the domain up to a null set. Taking logarithms gives the first inequality. Factorials, measure masses, and sheet sums are included in its uniform error.</p><p>Repeated nonzero \\(x_i\\) give zero original row determinants and are also covered by continuity of the interpolation estimate; repeated \\(s_j\\) give zero Vandermondes. Zero or boundary nodes form null sets. There the original integral expression supplies the integrable definition. All arbitrarily close interior configurations remain in the suprema; no separation condition is used.</p><p>The substitutions give\n\\[\n|t-t'|=\\frac{2|x-x'|(1-xx')}{(1+x^2)(1+(x')^2)},\n\\]\n\\[\n1-ts=\\frac{1-2xs+x^2}{1+x^2},\\qquad\n1-t=\\frac{(1-x)^2}{1+x^2}.\n\\]\nIn either majorant, the exponent of \\(|x_i|\\) is exactly\n\\[\n(C-1)+g-(n-1)=C+g-n=a+2g;\n\\]\nthere is no singular finite-size correction at \\(x=0\\). Keeping all other powers gives the stated identity and correction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 8 and the averaging notation of Lemma 8.1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(u=\\cos\\theta\\), \\(u'=\\cos\\theta'\\), define, for \\(0\\leq r&lt;1\\),\n  \\[\n  L_r(u,u')=\n  -\\log2+\\log|1-re^{i(\\theta+\\theta')}|\n  +\\log|1-re^{i(\\theta-\\theta')}|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/8\\), and put\n  \\[\n  \\tau=1-\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n  \\rho(x)=1-\\varepsilon(1-x),\\qquad\n  \\sigma(s)=1-\\varepsilon\\sqrt{1-s}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  A_k=\\langle\\tau^kT_k(x)\\rangle,\\quad\n  B_k=\\langle\\rho(x)^kx^k\\rangle,\\quad\n  C_k=\\langle\\sigma(s)^kT_k(s)\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 6, equations (81)–(87)).\nReplace the \\(x\\)-cosine kernel by \\(L_{\\tau^2}\\), the \\(s\\)-cosine kernel by \\(L_{\\sigma(s)\\sigma(s')}\\), the power kernel by\n\\[\n\\log(1-\\rho(x)\\rho(x')xx'),\n\\]\nand the cross kernel by\n\\[\n-\\log|1-\\rho(x)\\sigma(s)xe^{i\\arccos s}|^2.\n\\]\nEvery original kernel in Lemma 8.1 is bounded above by its replacement plus \\(O(\\varepsilon)\\), with an absolute configuration-independent constant. Including all diagonals, the damped interactions satisfy\n\\[\n\\frac\\kappa2\\langle L_{\\tau^2}(x,x')\\rangle\n=-\\frac\\kappa2\\log2-\\kappa\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{A_k^2}{k},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\frac12\\langle\\log(1-\\rho(x)\\rho(x')xx')\\rangle\n+\\langle L_{\\sigma(s)\\sigma(s')}(s,s')\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;\\hspace{12mm}\n-\\langle\\log|1-\\rho(x)\\sigma(s)xe^{i\\arccos s}|^2\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad=-\\log2-\\frac12\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{(B_k-2C_k)^2}{k}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nRemoving the added diagonals costs at most\n\\[\nO_\\varepsilon(1/n)\n-\\frac1{2n}\\langle\\log(1-x)\\rangle\n-\\frac1n\\langle\\log(1-s)\\rangle.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The logarithm power series and cosine addition formula give\n\\[\nL_r(u,u')=-\\log2\n-2\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{r^kT_k(u)T_k(u')}{k}.\n\\]\nAt distinct nodes, \\(r\\uparrow1\\) gives \\(L_1(u,u')=\\log|u-u'|\\), because the product of chord lengths is \\(2|u-u'|\\). Also, for \\(|z|,|z'|&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\log(1-zz')=-\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{z^k(z')^k}{k},\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\log(1-2zs+z^2)=2\\sum_{k\\geq1}\\frac{z^kT_k(s)}k.\n\\]\nThe last logarithm is real, since its argument is\n\\[\n|1-ze^{i\\arccos s}|^2.\n\\]\nThe matched damping preserves the moments needed to complete a square. Near \\((x,s)=(1,1)\\),\n\\[\n|1-xe^{i\\arccos s}|^2=(1-x)^2+2x(1-s),\n\\]\nwhich identifies the scales in \\(\\rho,\\sigma\\).</p><p>For \\(|\\zeta|=1\\), \\(0&lt;r\\leq1\\),\n\\[\n|1-r\\zeta|^2-r|1-\\zeta|^2=(1-r)^2\\geq0.\n\\]\nThus each cosine comparison costs at most \\(-\\log r\\), and \\(r\\geq(1-\\varepsilon)^2\\).</p><p>For the power kernel, put \\(u=xx'\\), \\(r=\\rho(x)\\rho(x')\\). If \\(u\\geq0\\), monotonicity gives\n\\[\n\\log(1-u)\\leq\\log(1-ru).\n\\]\nIf \\(u&lt;0\\), put \\(q=-u\\leq1\\); then\n\\[\n\\log(1+q)-\\log(1+rq)\n\\leq\\frac{(1-r)q}{1+rq}\\leq1-r\\leq4\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p><p>For the cross kernel, write\n\\[\na=1-x,\\quad b=\\sqrt{1-s},\\quad\\theta=\\arccos s,\\quad\nz=1-xe^{i\\theta},\\quad\nz_\\varepsilon=1-\\rho(x)\\sigma(s)xe^{i\\theta}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n|z_\\varepsilon-z|\\leq\\varepsilon|x|(a+b).\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\leq0\\), \\(|z|\\geq1\\), \\(|x|(a+b)\\leq3\\). For \\(0\\leq x\\leq1/2\\), \\(|z|\\geq1/2\\), \\(|x|(a+b)\\leq1\\). For \\(1/2\\leq x&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n|z|^2=a^2+2xb^2\\geq a^2+b^2,\\qquad\n|x|(a+b)\\leq\\sqrt2|z|.\n\\]\nThus globally \\(|z_\\varepsilon-z|\\leq3\\varepsilon|z|\\), and\n\\[\n-\\log|z|^2\n\\leq-\\log|z_\\varepsilon|^2+2\\log(1+3\\varepsilon)\n\\leq-\\log|z_\\varepsilon|^2+6\\varepsilon.\n\\]</p><p>For each fixed finite interior configuration, the damped series converge absolutely. Substitution gives the two square identities. The \\(B_k,C_k\\) are exactly the same in the pure and cross interactions. Combining their constants with \\(c_{\\kappa,n}\\log2\\) leaves\n\\[\n\\left(-1+\\alpha+\\gamma-\\frac{\\kappa+1}{2n}\\right)\\log2.\n\\]</p><p>It remains to control omitted diagonals before taking a supremum. A damped cosine diagonal is at least\n\\[\n-\\log2+2\\log(1-r).\n\\]\nThe \\(x\\)-cosine diagonals therefore cost \\(O_\\varepsilon(1/n)\\). For the power diagonal,\n\\[\n1-\\rho(x)^2x^2\\geq1-x\\quad(x\\geq0),\n\\]\n\\[\n1-\\rho(x)^2x^2\\geq1-(1-\\varepsilon)^2\\geq\\varepsilon\n\\quad(x\\leq0).\n\\]\nSince \\(1\\leq1-x\\leq2\\) for \\(x\\leq0\\), the correction is at most\n\\[\nO_\\varepsilon(1/n)-\\frac1{2n}\\langle\\log(1-x)\\rangle.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\n1-\\sigma(s)^2\n=\\varepsilon\\sqrt{1-s}\\bigl(2-\\varepsilon\\sqrt{1-s}\\bigr)\n\\geq\\varepsilon\\sqrt{1-s}.\n\\]\nThe \\(s\\)-cosine diagonals cost at most\n\\[\nO_\\varepsilon(1/n)-\\frac1n\\langle\\log(1-s)\\rangle.\n\\]\nThe cross term already contains all pairs. There is no loss involving \\(\\log|x|\\) or \\(\\log(1+x)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\Delta_N\\) from Proposition 1 without a rationality assumption.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(W_\\kappa,D,T,S,\\|\\cdot\\|_*\\) from Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Every finite decimal in this proposition and its lemmas denotes an exact rational number.</p><p class=\"li\">In case \\(2\\), take \\(d=10\\), \\(\\lambda_2=0\\); in case \\(1\\), take \\(d=8\\), \\(\\lambda_1=2.47405979\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Specify each trial sequence by\n  \\[\n  u_k=l_k+\\sum_zr_zz^k,\\qquad l_k=0\\quad(k&gt;d).\n  \\]\n  All integers in the following coefficient lists are multiplied by \\(10^{-8}\\); finite coefficients are in increasing order of \\(k\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|l}\n  \\kappa&amp;u&amp;10^8(l_1,\\ldots,l_d)\\\\ \\hline\n  2&amp;p&amp;\n  (45559127,-50750856,-6578767,13970217,4786184,\\\\\n  &amp;&amp;-4292433,-576704,1311615,671564,-346453)\\\\\n  2&amp;v&amp;\n  (-23910158,21152432,-2885110,-11558199,6485289,\\\\\n  &amp;&amp;1456821,-1912176,-2263524,2742210,-1162454)\\\\\n  1&amp;p&amp;\n  (11913521,-79993701,-21956443,37903579,10744022,\\\\\n  &amp;&amp;-2073193,570246,-24939103)\\\\\n  1&amp;v&amp;\n  (-89913025,52874280,45168341,-30708629,-19269841,\\\\\n  &amp;&amp;33922112,9819141,-16992389)\n  \\end{array}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Case \\(1\\) has no exponential terms. For case \\(2\\), the following table specifies them. For a real base, \\(10^{-8}a\\) is its coefficient. For a nonreal base, \\(10^{-8}a,10^{-8}b\\) multiply \\(\\operatorname{Re}(z^k),\\operatorname{Im}(z^k)\\); equivalently \\(r_z=10^{-8}(a-ib)/2\\), \\(r_{\\bar z}=\\overline{r_z}\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|r|r}\n  u&amp;z&amp;a&amp;b\\\\ \\hline\n  p&amp;.85&amp;-9338452&amp;\\\\\n  &amp;.94&amp;-2141509&amp;\\\\\n  &amp;.7i&amp;-66277922&amp;-31907569\\\\\n  &amp;.85i&amp;-1231651&amp;6002645\\\\\n  &amp;.092+.92i&amp;-3225918&amp;8928234\\\\\n  &amp;-.092+.92i&amp;-2105536&amp;-9091287\\\\ \\hline\n  v&amp;-.8&amp;15199211&amp;\\\\\n  &amp;-.96&amp;4451662&amp;\\\\\n  &amp;.88&amp;2545398&amp;\\\\\n  &amp;.95&amp;-4932634&amp;\\\\\n  &amp;.984&amp;11618157&amp;\\\\\n  &amp;.78i&amp;27238714&amp;-38447936\\\\\n  &amp;.9i&amp;-31341084&amp;-30188786\\\\\n  &amp;.955i&amp;-6693542&amp;11912254\\\\\n  &amp;.984i&amp;2055213&amp;-21715849\n  \\end{array}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  X_\\kappa(x)=W_\\kappa(x)+\\lambda_\\kappa D(x)\n  -2\\kappa T(p,x)-S(v,x),\n  \\quad x\\in[-1,1)\\setminus\\{0\\},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  Y_\\kappa(x)=\\beta\\log x+\\gamma\\log(1-x)+2T(v,x),\n  \\quad0&lt;x&lt;1.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Proposition 7.1).\nThe real-place bound is\n\\[\n\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\n\\left(\\frac{\\log|\\Delta_N|}{n^2}-\\frac12\\log2\\right)\n\\leq-2.290939875&lt;-2.2909.\n\\]\nThe separate majorant bounds in cases \\(2,1\\) are respectively\n\\[\n-2.290939875,\\qquad -2.296789875.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 8 with the exact trial sequences. Since\n\\[\n-1+\\alpha+\\gamma=-11/16,\n\\]\nuse Lemma 9.2, add \\(.000024\\) to each supremum by Lemma 9.3, and use \\(\\log2&gt;.693146\\). The case \\(2\\) right side is strictly less than\n\\[\n-\\frac{11}{16}(.693146)\n+.77844-.98399-1.60890+.000048\n=-2.290939875.\n\\]\nThe case \\(1\\) right side is strictly less than\n\\[\n-\\frac{11}{16}(.693146)\n+.9321-1.3244-1.4280+.000048\n=-2.296789875.\n\\]\nTaking the larger constant proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the exact trial sequences and functions of Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\operatorname{Log}\\) be the principal complex logarithm.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  t_u(x)=\\sum_{k=1}^dl_kU_{k-1}(x)\n  +\\sum_z\\frac{r_zz}{1-2xz+z^2},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  h_u(x)=\\sum_{k=1}^dl_kx^{k-1}\n  +\\sum_z\\frac{r_zz}{1-xz}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define rational polynomials by\n  \\[\n  Q_X(x)=x(1-x)(1+x^2)^{3-\\kappa}\n  \\prod_{z\\text{ in }p}(1-2xz+z^2)\n  \\prod_{z\\text{ in }v}(1-xz),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  Q_Y(x)=x(1-x)\\prod_{z\\text{ in }v}(1-2xz+z^2),\n  \\qquad\n  A_X=Q_XX_\\kappa',\\quad A_Y=Q_YY_\\kappa'.\n  \\]\n  Conjugates are counted separately; empty products are one.</p><p>Statement (paper, Sections 7.1–7.2).\nThe trial sequences are real and exponentially decaying. Their functions have the finite expressions\n\\[\n\\|u\\|_*^2=\n\\sum_{k=1}^d\\frac{l_k^2+2l_k\\sum_zr_zz^k}{k}\n-\\sum_{z,z'}r_zr_{z'}\\operatorname{Log}(1-zz'),\n\\]\n\\[\nT(u,x)=\\sum_{k=1}^d\\frac{l_kT_k(x)}k\n-\\frac12\\sum_zr_z\\operatorname{Log}(1-2xz+z^2),\n\\]\n\\[\nS(u,x)=\\sum_{k=1}^d\\frac{l_kx^k}k\n-\\sum_zr_z\\operatorname{Log}(1-xz).\n\\]\nAll stationary points of \\(X_\\kappa,Y_\\kappa\\) in their open domains are exhausted by the rational root brackets specified in the proof, together with possible roots at the division points specified there.</p>", "proof": "<p>Counting conjugates, there are ten \\(p\\)-tail terms and thirteen \\(v\\)-tail terms, with base moduli at most \\(.94,.984\\). Every \\(|l_k|,|r_z|&lt;1\\). Reality follows by conjugation, and exponential decay is immediate.</p><p>The logarithm power series gives the finite expressions. Norm and power log arguments have positive real part. For the cosine term, with \\(x=\\cos\\theta\\),\n\\[\n1-2xz+z^2=(1-ze^{i\\theta})(1-ze^{-i\\theta}).\n\\]\nBoth factors have positive real part; their principal arguments sum strictly in \\((-\\pi,\\pi)\\). Their logarithms therefore sum to the principal logarithm of the product, even when the product has negative real part.</p><p>Differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_\\kappa'(x)={}&amp;\n\\frac{\\alpha+2\\gamma}{x}-\\frac{2\\eta}{1-x}\n-(\\kappa+2\\alpha+2\\eta+2\\gamma)\\frac{x}{1+x^2}\\\\\n&amp;-\\frac{4\\lambda_\\kappa x}{(1+x^2)^2}\n-2\\kappa t_p(x)-h_v(x),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\\[\nY_\\kappa'(x)=\\frac\\beta x-\\frac\\gamma{1-x}+2t_v(x).\n\\]\nThese and the coefficient tables specify \\(A_X,A_Y\\) over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), using the Chebyshev recurrences. The denominators do not vanish for real \\(|x|\\leq1\\):\n\\[\n|1-xz|\\geq1-|z|&gt;0,\\qquad\n|1-2xz+z^2|\\geq(1-|z|)^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nConjugate factors have positive products, and real-base factors are positive. Thus \\(Q_X\\) has the sign of \\(x\\), and \\(Q_Y&gt;0\\) on \\((0,1)\\).</p><p>For a specified numerator \\(A(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^{d_0}A_jx^j\\), on an interval \\((b,c)\\), form\n\\[\n(1+t)^{d_0}A\\left(\\frac{b+ct}{1+t}\\right)\n=\\sum_{h=0}^{d_0}a_ht^h,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\na_h=\\sum_{j=0}^{d_0}\\sum_{k=0}^{j}\nA_j\\binom jk b^{j-k}c^k\n\\binom{d_0-j}{h-k}.\n\\]\nOut-of-range binomials are zero. Delete zero coefficients and count sign changes. The exact results are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|l|l}\n\\kappa&amp;\\text{function}&amp;d_0&amp;\\text{division points}&amp;\\text{variations}\\\\ \\hline\n2&amp;X&amp;36&amp;-1,0,1&amp;9,9\\\\\n2&amp;Y&amp;24&amp;0,.25,.5,.75,1&amp;5,2,2,6\\\\\n1&amp;X&amp;13&amp;-1,-.5,0,.5,1&amp;1,1,2,1\\\\\n1&amp;Y&amp;9&amp;0,1&amp;5\n\\end{array}.\n\\]</p><p>Descartes’ rule bounds positive roots with multiplicity. The needed bound follows by factoring out positive roots: multiplication by \\(t-r\\), \\(r&gt;0\\), increases variations by at least one. Rescale to \\(r=1\\) and remove zero initial coefficients. If \\(a_0,\\ldots,a_d\\) are old coefficients and \\(b_0,\\ldots,b_{d+1}\\) the new ones, then\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^hb_j=-a_h\\quad(0\\leq h\\leq d),\n\\qquad b_{d+1}=a_d.\n\\]\nEach sign change of nonzero partial sums forces an intervening \\(b_j\\) of the later sign. Starting at \\(b_0=-a_0\\), these give the old variations, and \\(b_{d+1}\\), opposite to the last partial sum, gives one more. Factoring successively proves the bound. The substitution maps \\(t&gt;0\\) bijectively to \\((b,c)\\), preserving multiplicities.</p><p>The following integers \\(m\\) specify disjoint brackets\n\\[\n(m,m+2)/10^{10}.\n\\]\nFor \\(X_2\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n-9601109148,\\ -8942317572,\\ -7608305633,\\ -6503394794,\\\\\n-5185864065,\\ -4015634158,\\ -3108806646,\\ -2067921826,\\\\\n-1589849496,\\ 1531948062,\\ 2072448179,\\ 3208186484,\\\\\n4381119427,\\ 5851354199,\\ 7269030693,\\ 8390277402,\\\\\n9332614564,\\ 9709786219.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nFor \\(Y_2\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n176402802,\\ 330649406,\\ 764952882,\\ 1227250753,\\\\\n2149465998,\\ 3048189112,\\ 4322699096,\\ 5564757994,\\\\\n6801929373,\\ 8031988371,\\ 8877037851,\\ 9577761832,\\\\\n9838463999,\\ 9972727815,\\ 9992037196.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nFor \\(X_1\\):\n\\[\n-9917299785,\\ -2259572153,\\ 2543808026,\\ 4437270259,\\ 6348298970.\n\\]\nFor \\(Y_1\\):\n\\[\n532669786,\\ 2504239325,\\ 5701738806,\\ 7966939383,\\ 9454490138.\n\\]</p><p>For every listed \\(m\\), the exact sign check is\n\\[\nN_A(m)N_A(m+2)&lt;0,\n\\qquad\nN_A(s)=\\sum_{j=0}^{d_0}A_j(10^{10})^{d_0-j}s^j.\n\\]\nThese are rational addition-and-multiplication recipes. In each division interval, the number of brackets equals its variation count. The intermediate value theorem and the variation bound therefore give exactly one simple root per bracket and no others in the open intervals. Possible roots at division points are treated by direct value evaluation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 9 and Lemma 9.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Candidate evaluation points are the left endpoints of all brackets in Lemma 9.1, together with:\n  \\[\n  -1\\text{ for }X_2;\\qquad\n  1/4,1/2,3/4\\text{ for }Y_2;\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  -1,-1/2,1/2\\text{ for }X_1.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 7.3, Table 2 and equation (98)).\nExact rational logarithm calculations certify\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\kappa&amp;\n\\kappa\\|p\\|_*^2+\\frac12\\|v\\|_*^2&amp;\nX_\\kappa\\text{ at candidates}&amp;Y_\\kappa\\text{ at candidates}\\\\ \\hline\n2&amp;&lt;.77844&amp;&lt;-.98399&amp;&lt;-1.60890\\\\\n1&amp;&lt;.9321&amp;&lt;-1.3244&amp;&lt;-1.4280\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n.693146&lt;\\log2&lt;.693149.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a positive rational \\(s\\), write \\(s=2^my\\), \\(1\\leq y\\leq2\\), and put\n\\[\nH(y)=2\\sum_{j=0}^{17}\\frac1{2j+1}\n\\left(\\frac{y-1}{y+1}\\right)^{2j+1}.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(mH(2)+H(y)\\) for \\(\\log s\\). With \\(q=(y-1)/(y+1)\\),\n\\[\n0\\leq\\log y-H(y)\\leq\\frac{2q^{37}}{37(1-q^2)}.\n\\]</p><p>For a nonzero rational complex number \\(a+ib\\), its real log part is half the real log of \\(a^2+b^2\\). For its principal argument, rotate by \\(-k\\pi/4\\), \\(-4\\leq k\\leq4\\), to positive real part and imaginary-to-real ratio \\(t\\) with \\(|t|\\leq1/2\\), choosing the principal argument \\(k\\pi/4+\\arctan t\\); at the negative real axis, use \\(\\pi\\). The ratio is rational, since, up to positive scale, rotations use repeated maps\n\\[\n(a,b)\\mapsto(a+b,b-a),\\qquad\n(a,b)\\mapsto(a-b,a+b).\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nJ(t)=\\sum_{j=0}^{23}\\frac{(-1)^jt^{2j+1}}{2j+1}.\n\\]\nSubstitute\n\\[\nk\\bigl(J(1/2)+J(1/3)\\bigr)+J(t).\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\n\\arctan(1/2)+\\arctan(1/3)=\\pi/4\n\\]\nfollows from tangent addition and the range of the sum. The error is bounded by \\(|t|^{49}/49\\).</p><p>All positive inputs, including squared moduli, lie between \\(2^{-100}\\) and \\(2^{100}\\): real arguments at evaluation points lie between \\(.0007\\) and \\(2\\); squared complex moduli lie between \\((.016)^4\\) and \\((1+.984)^4\\). Thus at most \\(100\\) scaling steps suffice. Put\n\\[\nh_0=\\frac{2(1/3)^{37}}{37(1-1/9)},\\qquad\nj_0=\\frac{(1/2)^{49}}{49}.\n\\]\nA real log costs at most \\(101h_0\\), and an argument at most \\(9j_0\\). Since\n\\[\n2\\cdot101h_0+9j_0&lt;7\\cdot10^{-16},\n\\]\n\\(10^{-15}\\) bounds a complex-log error; this also gives the coarser \\(10^{-11}\\) allowance.</p><p>Evaluate rational polynomial terms exactly. Total absolute log-coefficient masses are at most \\(284.5\\) for\n\\(\\kappa\\|p\\|_*^2+\\|v\\|_*^2/2\\), less than \\(35\\) for \\(X_\\kappa\\), and less than \\(14\\) for \\(Y_\\kappa\\). Their errors are below\n\\[\n2.85\\cdot10^{-13},\\qquad3.5\\cdot10^{-14},\\qquad1.4\\cdot10^{-14},\n\\]\neach below \\(10^{-8}\\). One may enclose rational series terms between consecutive multiples of \\(10^{-40}\\), include remainders outwardly, and propagate intervals linearly. The rounding change is below \\(10^{-32}\\). For complex weights, use\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}(c\\operatorname{Log}z)\n=\\operatorname{Re}(c)\\operatorname{Re}(\\operatorname{Log}z)\n-\\operatorname{Im}(c)\\operatorname{Im}(\\operatorname{Log}z),\n\\]\nretaining coefficient signs.</p><p>The resulting point-value upper bounds, rounded upward to twelve decimal places after remainders, are listed below. In each pair, the first number is \\(10^{10}x\\). A star marks a division point; unmarked entries are bracket left endpoints.</p><p>For \\(X_2\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|r}\n-9601109148&amp;-.984034048775\\\\\n-8942317572&amp;-.984375363807\\\\\n-7608305633&amp;-.984034052640\\\\\n-6503394794&amp;-.984105522615\\\\\n-5185864065&amp;-.984034053414\\\\\n-4015634158&amp;-.984092061375\\\\\n-3108806646&amp;-.984034037901\\\\\n-2067921826&amp;-.984364031075\\\\\n-1589849496&amp;-.984034038450\\\\\n1531948062&amp;-.984033385315\\\\\n2072448179&amp;-.984776982913\\\\\n3208186484&amp;-.984034026898\\\\\n4381119427&amp;-.984264010107\\\\\n5851354199&amp;-.984034029391\\\\\n7269030693&amp;-.984245044505\\\\\n8390277402&amp;-.984034016294\\\\\n9332614564&amp;-.984567630027\\\\\n9709786219&amp;-.984033926884\\\\\n-10000000000^*&amp;-.986727371546\n\\end{array}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(Y_2\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|r}\n176402802&amp;-1.608946411646\\\\\n330649406&amp;-1.609949505120\\\\\n764952882&amp;-1.608960295828\\\\\n1227250753&amp;-1.609094597854\\\\\n2149465998&amp;-1.608960426500\\\\\n3048189112&amp;-1.608992193672\\\\\n4322699096&amp;-1.608960428486\\\\\n5564757994&amp;-1.608979509517\\\\\n6801929373&amp;-1.608960430478\\\\\n8031988371&amp;-1.608990548812\\\\\n8877037851&amp;-1.608960429811\\\\\n9577761832&amp;-1.609055802895\\\\\n9838463999&amp;-1.608960412835\\\\\n9972727815&amp;-1.609694899071\\\\\n9992037196&amp;-1.608958123669\\\\\n2500000000^*&amp;-1.608973617030\\\\\n5000000000^*&amp;-1.608971701898\\\\\n7500000000^*&amp;-1.608976908052\n\\end{array}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(X_1\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|r}\n-9917299785&amp;-2.778491531574\\\\\n-2259572153&amp;-1.324666731948\\\\\n2543808026&amp;-1.324655807046\\\\\n4437270259&amp;-1.352236629957\\\\\n6348298970&amp;-1.324666329425\\\\\n-10000000000^*&amp;-2.775818077526\\\\\n-5000000000^*&amp;-1.544057819905\\\\\n5000000000^*&amp;-1.347557680876\n\\end{array}.\n\\]\nFor \\(Y_1\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|r}\n532669786&amp;-1.428286151250\\\\\n2504239325&amp;-1.515602647362\\\\\n5701738806&amp;-1.428335732372\\\\\n7966939383&amp;-1.465686164672\\\\\n9454490138&amp;-1.428335358167\n\\end{array}.\n\\]\nThe largest unrounded interval width is less than\n\\[\n2.16146856249281\\cdot10^{-16}.\n\\]</p><p>The same calculation gives norm upper bounds\n\\[\n.778415976284\\quad(\\kappa=2),\\qquad\n.931985203901\\quad(\\kappa=1).\n\\]\nBefore adding error allowances, convenient strict cutoffs are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\n\\kappa&amp;\\text{norm}&amp;X&amp;Y\\\\ \\hline\n2&amp;.77843&amp;-.98400&amp;-1.60891\\\\\n1&amp;.93205&amp;-1.32442&amp;-1.42805\n\\end{array}.\n\\]\nAdding the \\(10^{-8}\\) allowance gives the weaker bounds stated in the lemma. The same logarithm procedure gives the asserted bounds for \\(\\log2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit Proposition 9 and the root brackets of Lemma 9.1.</p><p>Statement (paper, Section 7.4).\nThroughout every whole closed root bracket,\n\\[\n|X_\\kappa'|&lt;120000,\\qquad |Y_\\kappa'|&lt;120000.\n\\]\nConsequently, the value at a bracket’s stationary point exceeds the value at its left endpoint by less than \\(.000024\\). The candidate values of Lemma 9.2, with this allowance, bound the global suprema of \\(X_\\kappa,Y_\\kappa\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>All bracket points are farther than \\(.0007\\) from both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). In particular, the right endpoint nearest \\(s=1\\) is\n\\[\n9992037198/10^{10},\n\\]\nat distance exactly \\(.0007962802\\).</p><p>For \\(|x|\\leq1\\),\n\\[\nU_{k-1}(\\cos\\theta)=\\frac{\\sin(k\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta}\n\\]\ngives \\(|U_{k-1}(x)|\\leq k\\), including endpoint limits. The polynomial part of \\(X_\\kappa'\\) is at most\n\\[\n4\\sum_{k=1}^{10}k+10=230.\n\\]\nIts endpoint terms are bounded by\n\\[\n(19/48+1/12)/.0007&lt;685,\n\\]\nits \\(x/(1+x^2)\\) term is below \\(3\\), and its multiplier term below \\(4\\lambda_1&lt;10\\). Its log-tail derivatives are bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac{4\\cdot10}{(.06)^2}+\\frac{13}{.016}&lt;11924.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n|X_\\kappa'|&lt;12852&lt;120000.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(Y_\\kappa'\\), the polynomial part is at most \\(110\\), endpoint terms at most\n\\[\n(7/48+1/12)/.0007&lt;328,\n\\]\nand log tails at most\n\\[\n26/(.016)^2=101562.5.\n\\]\nThus \\(|Y_\\kappa'|&lt;102001&lt;120000\\). These bounds cover both cases; tails are empty in case \\(1\\).</p><p>The mean value theorem gives an increase less than\n\\[\n120000\\cdot\\frac2{10^{10}}=.000024.\n\\]\nLemma 9.1 exhausts stationary points except possible division-point roots, all of which are evaluated in Lemma 9.2. The regular endpoint \\(-1\\) is also evaluated for \\(X_\\kappa\\). The remaining endpoint limits are \\(-\\infty\\):\n\\[\nx\\to0^\\pm,\\ x\\to1^-\\quad\\text{for }X_\\kappa,\n\\]\n\\[\nx\\to0^+,\\ x\\to1^-\\quad\\text{for }Y_\\kappa.\n\\]\nBounded tangent and multiplier terms do not change these limits. Thus the certificate controls global suprema, not merely sampled values.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 14, "cite"], [3, 19, "cite"], [3, 23, "cite"], [3, 26, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [5, 10, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 0, "cite"], [9, 17, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 9, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 14, "cite"], [18, 14, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [19, 22, "cite"], [19, 23, "cite"], [19, 24, "cite"], [20, 22, "cite"], [20, 24, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [22, 24, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [27, 29, "cite"], [28, 26, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 26, "cite"]]});
